RE_NAME-$$\", but an\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a unique name instead.\n# FORMAL_WORK_ID pins the suffix so tools/eval/formal.py can remove exactly\n# its own dir afterwards. genchecks.py derives @core\n…[17696 of 20768 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:cd567b4999058c46]…\nDI x2, x1, 3 — rs1 forward from MEM/WB stage.\"\"\"\n    program = [ADDI(1, 0, 5), NOP(), ADDI(2, 1, 3), EBREAK()]\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, program)\n    [r2] = _by_insn(rets, ADDI(2, 1, 3))\n    assert r2[\"rd_wdata\"] == 8\n\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +735.07%): Moving combinational DIV/REM (`/` and `%`) out of the shared ALU into a registered iterative divider removes their timing cost from ordinary instructions, letting Fmax gains outweigh CoreMark's added divide stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (regression, +7.45%): Registering elastic IF/ID removes redirect-to-decode feedback, but the added taken-branch refill cycle raised CoreMark cycles 6.45%, limiting a 14.39% Fmax gain to 7.45% fitness.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 (improvement, +13.63%): Instruction-qualified BHT/JAL prediction removes recurring CoreMark control bubbles, while saved direct targets enable direction-XOR recovery and full ID/EX control clearing keeps correction out of decode/register-file read paths.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
