{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '530,550p' tools/orchestrator.py; git diff --check; git status --short; sed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"", "aggregated_output": "    if build.returncode != 0:\n        return _fail(\"cosim build\", build)\n    return (True, \"\")\n\ndef _read_notes(worktree: str) -> str:\n    p = Path(worktree) / \"implementation_notes.md\"\n    return p.read_text() if p.exists() else \"\"\n\ndef run_report():\n    log = read_log()\n    if not log:\n        print(\"No experiments yet.\")\n        return\n    improvements = [e for e in log if e.get('outcome') == 'improvement']\n    broken       = [e for e in log if e.get('outcome') == 'broken']\n    regressions  = [e for e in log if e.get('outcome') == 'regression']\n    print(f\"\\nExperiment Report\")\n    print(f\"  Total iterations : {len(log)}\")\n    print(f\"  Improvements     : {len(improvements)}\")\n    print(f\"  Regressions      : {len(regressions)}\")\n    print(f\"  Broken           : {len(broken)}\")\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n?? cores/bench/test/test_hazard_unit.py\n# Implementation notes\n\nChanged the hazard-unit equations exactly as proposed:\n\n- `stall_if = dmem_stall || ex_busy || load_use_hazard || imem_stall`\n- `stall_id = dmem_stall || ex_busy || load_use_hazard`\n\nThe flush equations and downstream holds are unchanged. Added comments at the\nhazard unit and destination registers documenting reset/redirect/stall PC\npriority and complete control/valid/prediction flush priority over ID stall.\nIF and ID register logic, predictor, execution, memory, and RVFI are unchanged.\n\nRetained all existing tests. Strengthened pipeline checks to verify PC priority\non every edge and asserted fetch stall during unavailable-imem corrections,\nincluding after older memory holds and load-use bubbles. Added a trained loop\nexit with older memory stalls, load-use bubbles, blocking division, exact RVFI\nPCs/results/order/traps, once-per-advance training, and accepted memory effects.\nAdded an exhaustive hazard-unit test covering 1,152 input combinations, including\nsynthetic simultaneous redirect/load-use and older holds. Existing ID-stage\ntests check every stall/flush/valid combination, full control clearing, and\npayload retention. No architectural deviations from the hypothesis.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings.\n- `make bench` then `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: PASS, all five ELFs;\n  unstalled/stalled selftest and three random-program RVFI comparisons, plus\n  canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/out-of-bounds validation.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\n  PASS, 53 checks passed, 0 failed, first attempt; auto-selected nret=1 wrapper\n  and ALTOPS configuration. No counterexamples or RTL fix attempts.\n- Full cocotb suite: PASS, all 14 pytest runners / 117 cocotb cases, including\n  real RV32M and ALTOPS tests and the new trained-exit and exhaustive hazard cases.\n- Gowin timing: all three canonical placement options successfully routed.\n  Fmax: option 0 = 83.220 MHz, option 1 = 87.205 MHz, option 2 = 87.205 MHz;\n  median = 87.205 MHz. Logic = 3,720; registers = 1,090.\n- Canonical CoreMark: 10 iterations, exactly 4,296,444 marker-bounded cycles,\n  2.3275061888389562e-6 iterations/cycle, matching the champion's cycle count.\n- Fitness = 202.97 iterations/second, below the required 228.40456. The\n  hypothesis did not achieve the timing/fitness acceptance threshold.\n\nThe median-option worst path remains 38 cells from registered EX rs2 through\nEX logic named `redirect_Z`, ID operand selection (`rs2_value`), and the ID\nALU-input register's D pin. The stall equations no longer depend on correction,\nbut a competing operand-selection path remains.