{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r1s0.yaml cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261002-001-r1s0.last'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Share a width-tapered combinational divider across RV32M operations\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline's worst timed path crosses forwarding and the ALU, reaching\n  199.837 ns through 156 cells at a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. alu.sv expresses\n  DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU as separate signed/unsigned division and remainder\n  operators. Their combinational implementation is the strongest suspected\n  source of the deep arithmetic path and substantial LUT usage. A smaller,\n  explicitly shared divider can improve the timed circuit even when CoreMark\n  is executing other operations, because the full RV32IM hardware is timed.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with one combinational\n  unsigned restoring divider that produces quotient and remainder together.\n  Keep the existing pipeline, forwarding and bus-handshake behavior, with\n  no extra cycles for any instruction. For signed operations, convert both\n  operands to unsigned 32-bit magnitudes before the divider, then negate the\n  quotient when operand signs differ and negate the remainder only when the\n  dividend is negative. Unsigned operations use the original operand bits.\n\n  Make the 32 unrolled restoring stages explicitly width-tapered. At stage\n  k=1..32, append dividend bit 32-k to the previous remainder to form a\n  k-bit trial remainder. Subtract divisor[k-1:0] once using a k+1-bit\n  zero-extended subtraction. Accept its low k bits and set quotient bit\n  32-k only when the subtraction has no borrow and every divisor bit above\n  k-1 is zero; otherwise retain the trial remainder and emit quotient zero.\n  At k=32 the upper-divisor predicate is constant true. Use the subtraction's\n  borrow bit instead of a separate magnitude comparator, and use static\n  generate widths so early stages contain only the necessary carry logic.\n  The final stage supplies both results; do not infer another '%' circuit.\n\n  Preserve division-by-zero results explicitly (quotient 0xffffffff,\n  remainder original dividend) and signed INT_MIN/-1 results (quotient\n  0x80000000, remainder zero). Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions\n  exactly as they are and exclude the real divider from that build. Keep\n  multiply and all RV32I datapaths unchanged. The expected gain comes from\n  sharing arithmetic, reducing carry-chain width and avoiding duplicate\n  comparator/subtractor logic, with unchanged iterations per cycle.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 25\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the four real division/remainder expressions with one shared\n      unsigned quotient/remainder network using 32 statically sized restoring\n      stages. Add signed magnitude conversion, quotient/remainder sign\n      correction and explicit RV32M corner-case selection. Preserve the ALU\n      port contract, other operations and exact ALTOPS formulas; guard the\n      real divider with the non-ALTOPS branch. Keep all divider logic local\n      to this source file so source-list changes are unnecessary.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all current checks and add focused DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU vectors\n      around powers of two, divisors with bit 31 set, zero divisors,\n      INT_MIN/-1, and mixed signs. Exercise successive operation changes on\n      fixed operands to verify the shared result selection. Check both exact\n      RV32M results and a=q*b+r for nonzero unsigned divisors, with remainder\n      less than divisor. Run the existing ALU suite and the unchanged formal\n      and stalled CoreMark cosimulation gates before vendor timing evaluation.\nWrote and schema-validated the [hypothesis YAML](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_1-sol_xhigh-v2-rep6/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261002-001-r1s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r1s0.yaml).\n\nProposes a shared, width-tapered combinational divider with unchanged instruction latency. Expected fitness gain: **25%**, medium confidence. Arithmetic model checks passed; FPGA timing remains unmeasured.", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
