{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r1s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Move division out of the single-cycle execute path\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The only recorded bench experiment is the baseline: 5.442 MHz with a\n  199.837 ns worst path spanning forwarding, EX, and the combinational ALU\n  through 156 cells. There are no previous structural attempts or recorded\n  lessons. The ALU implements signed and unsigned divide and remainder with\n  combinational / and % operators. These unrolled arithmetic networks remain\n  in the timed result-selection cone even when an ordinary RV32I instruction\n  executes. Moving this rare, deep operation behind registers offers much\n  more timing headroom than improving an individual forwarding mux.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace all four combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU paths with one shared,\n  blocking, iterative divider connected to EX. Keep RV32I execution and\n  multiplication on their existing single-cycle paths. The divider captures\n  the fully forwarded operands and operation exactly once, converts signed\n  operands to unsigned magnitudes, and performs one radix-2 restoring\n  division step per cycle using a registered partial remainder, quotient,\n  divisor, and a bounded counter. Share the magnitude datapath between signed\n  and unsigned operations and between quotient and remainder; register sign\n  correction before presenting the result. A normal division takes about\n  34-35 cycles including preparation and completion. Explicitly implement\n  divide-by-zero and signed INT_MIN/-1 semantics with bounded early finish.\n\n  While the divider runs, hold the instruction in ID/EX and stop fetch/decode,\n  but let older EX/MEM and MEM/WB instructions drain. Insert invalid bubbles\n  into EX/MEM rather than freezing an already completed memory request or\n  replaying an older retirement. Clear reg_write, mem_read, mem_write, and\n  redirect controls in these bubbles as well as valid, because the current\n  memory and forwarding logic consumes control bits without validity gates.\n  An older dmem stall still holds EX/MEM and\n\n…[4431 of 7503 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4e473d8f32ddb25a]…\nchecks.\n  - file: test/test_div_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Test all migrated signed/unsigned divide and remainder vectors, random\n      operands, zero divisors, INT_MIN/-1, negative remainder signs, and\n      magnitude extremes. Check bounded completion, changing request inputs\n      while busy, held-result backpressure, consecutive accepted requests,\n      and reset during an operation.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add div_unit.sv to the existing pipeline runner's explicit RTL source\n      list and extend its cycle bounds for multi-cycle division. Add directed\n      retirement checks for ALU-to-divide forwarding, immediate\n      divide-result consumers, back-to-back divides, and divides following\n      stalled loads/stores under independent imem/dmem stalls. Verify exactly\n      one retirement per instruction, monotonically consecutive orders,\n      correct captured operands, and no duplicate memory side effects while\n      older instructions drain during divider occupancy.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
