eMark cycles, shifting the timing limit to ALU-result-to-ID forwarding.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r4s1 (improvement, +27.36%): Routing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU through a single registered response breaks the combinational EX-multiply-to-ID-operand forwarding recurrence, letting Fmax gains outweigh CoreMark's one-cycle-per-multiply penalty while preserving ordinary RV32I throughput.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r4s2 (regression, +7.42%): Compact validity-reset BHT banks complement operand retiming by reducing area and routing pressure, but XOR indexing cuts CoreMark cycles only 0.29%, leaving gains dominated by Fmax and the registered rs2_val-to-multiplier timing limit.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r5s0 (regression, -2.63%): Removing reset/flush/fetch-valid gating from the ID/EX payload leaves the divider-completion/forwarding-to-alu_a/alu_b critical path intact, so reduced control fanout alone can regress Fmax even with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r5s2 (improvement, +5.21%): Keeping full reset but limiting ID/EX flush/invalid-fetch clearing to ctrl_t, valid and predicted_taken while preserving payload stall holds reduces recovery muxing and fanout, improving Fmax and LUT use with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r6s0 (regression, -4.55%): Forcing shared AND/OR/XOR LUT4 boundaries with `syn_keep` achieved one LUT per bit but increased total logic from 3,675 to 3,714 while ALU/EX-to-ID selection remained critical, showing that reducing RTL case arms can regress Fmax without changing CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r6s1 (regression, -6.40%): A six-candidate one-hot mask-and-OR operand crossbar bypassed EX result muxes but increased LUTs from 3,675 to 3,887 while accepted-load forwarding and branch/predictor control limited timing, reducing Fmax from 93.817 to 87.809 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r6s2 (regression, -6.04%): One-bit agree writes eliminate the saturating-counter training lookup and reduce predictor area but leave EX-to-ID operand feedback critical; Fmax fell from 93.817 to 88.547 MHz while CoreMark cycles rose only 0.45%, showing that simpler training does not guarantee faster timing.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
