{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r5s0.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-001-r5s0',\n 'title':'Restrict ID/EX reset and squash to validity and control',\n 'category':'micro_opt',\n 'motivation':'''The current champion scores 207.55 iter/sec at 89.174 MHz. Its worst path\nreturns through EX completion and forwarding into ID/EX operand registers.\nThe current ID register places reset, flush and fetch validity on the entire\nwide payload, although invalid instructions are already suppressed by valid\nand fully cleared controls downstream. Prior micro_opt work identified this\nfanout before operand retiming and registered multiplication. The current\npipeline makes the same local control/data partition promising again.\n''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Partition the ID/EX register into a narrow reset/squash control bank and a\npayload bank that captures on \"'!stall. Remove reset, flush and in.valid from\npayload D and enable cones. Keep exactly the existing pipeline stages,\ninstruction latencies, forwarding, branch prediction and arithmetic units.\nThis is one micro-optimization: implement cancellation through instruction\ncontrol state rather than clearing every associated data bit.\n\nThe payload bank contains pc, architectural rs1/rs2 values, final alu_a/alu_b,\nimm, rd, rs1_addr, rs2_addr, instr, predicted_taken and direct_target. It has\nno reset or squash branch: on every !stall edge capture its existing\ncombinational next values; otherwise hold. It may capture unavailable or\nwrong-path fetched bits, but they cannot perform architectural work. Do not\nadd a flush-over-stall data update or change the existing hazard logic.\n\nKeep ctrl_t and valid in a separate always_ff block with their original\npriority: reset or flush clears the complete ctrl_t and valid, even when\nstall is asserted; otherwise !stall captures decoded controls and valid\nonly for an available instruction, and captures ctrl_t='\"'0 and valid=0 for\nan unavailable fetch. Otherwise retain controls and validity. Clearing all\nside-effect controls, rather than only valid, is essential because some\nconsumers inspect controls without qualifying them with validity.\n\nA valid instruction always captures every payload field on the same edge\nthat sets valid. A held valid instruction retains operands and metadata\nthrough load-use, older dmem backpressure and blocking arithmetic. Reset\nmust still clear all downstream EX/MEM and MEM/WB bundles, arithmetic unit\nstate, the register file, PC, predictor and retirement order as before.\nThe existing EX path clears the entire EX/MEM bundle for invalid input;\nthus uninitialized or squashed ID data cannot enter memory, bypass, branch\ntraining, requests, or valid RVFI records. Prove this with symbolic initial\npayload values and retain every reset and trap assertion. Do not introduce\nsynthesis don't-cares or relax any test to accept observable X/Z values.\n\nA reset-preserving prototype of the payload/control partition measured\n87.538, 92.250 and 92.250 MHz across Gowin placement options, versus the\nchampion's 83.021, 89.174 and 89.174 MHz. Its median gain was only 3.45\npercent, below the 4.6 percent acceptance margin. The proposed refinement\nalso removes reset from the payload bank, reducing the same control fanout\nand muxing further. That added reset removal must be timed and verified;\nthe earlier prototype does not establish an accepted improvement.\n\nRequire unchanged stalled and unstalled RVFI cosimulation, all unit checks,\nformal legality/ordering/liveness, canonical CoreMark CRCs and validation\nliteral, bounded memory accesses and the existing MMIO timing brackets.\nCoreMark cycle count should remain exactly 4,296,444 for the supplied\nstall sequence. With unchanged cycles, median Fmax must exceed about\n93.276 MHz to clear 217.0973 iter/sec. Preserve nret=1, core IO, decoder\ndefault-illegal behavior, trap suppression, arithmetic and all invariants.\n''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Split ID/EX sequential updates into payload and control/valid banks.\nCapture payload solely on \"'!stall with no reset, flush or fetch-valid\ncondition. Keep full ctrl_t/valid reset, flush and invalid-bubble clearing\nwith original priority, and hold the complete accepted instruction during\nstalls. Preserve all existing decode, operand forwarding and ALU-input\nselection expressions; add no pipeline stage or new interlock.\n'\"'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain all existing assertions. Add focused reset-during-hold, arithmetic\nwait, stalled-memory and wrong-path sequences with loads, stores, M ops,\nillegal instructions, branches and JALR. Verify that invalid/uninitialized\nID payload cannot create memory or arithmetic requests, forwarding writes,\nbranch training or RVFI retirements. Require exact accepted operands,\nconsecutive retirement order and unchanged zero-wait instruction latency.\nRun unchanged formal and stalled CoreMark gates; include a symbolic\ninitial-payload audit before relying on removal of payload reset.\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=85))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised single-angle hypothesis validates:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
