{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg --files -uu cores/bench/generated cores/bench/worktrees 2>/dev/null | head -60; rg -n 'probe|shift|carry|adder' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; [print(d[\\\"id\\\"],d.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\")[-3000:]) for x in sys.stdin for d in [json.loads(x.split(\\\":\\\",1)[1])]]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-001-r1s0 Replace only the real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with one combinational\nunsigned restoring divider that produces quotient and remainder together.\nKeep the existing pipeline, forwarding and bus-handshake behavior, with\nno extra cycles for any instruction. For signed operations, convert both\noperands to unsigned 32-bit magnitudes before the divider, then negate the\nquotient when operand signs differ and negate the remainder only when the\ndividend is negative. Unsigned operations use the original operand bits.\n\nMake the 32 unrolled restoring stages explicitly width-tapered. At stage\nk=1..32, append dividend bit 32-k to the previous remainder to form a\nk-bit trial remainder. Subtract divisor[k-1:0] once using a k+1-bit\nzero-extended subtraction. Accept its low k bits and set quotient bit\n32-k only when the subtraction has no borrow and every divisor bit above\nk-1 is zero; otherwise retain the trial remainder and emit quotient zero.\nAt k=32 the upper-divisor predicate is constant true. Use the subtraction's\nborrow bit instead of a separate magnitude comparator, and use static\ngenerate widths so early stages contain only the necessary carry logic.\nThe final stage supplies both results; do not infer another '%' circuit.\n\nPreserve division-by-zero results explicitly (quotient 0xffffffff,\nremainder original dividend) and signed INT_MIN/-1 results (quotient\n0x80000000, remainder zero). Keep the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions\nexactly as they are and exclude the real divider from that build. Keep\nmultiply and all RV32I datapaths unchanged. The expected gain comes from\nsharing arithmetic, reducing carry-chain width and avoiding duplicate\ncomparator/subtractor logic, with unchanged iterations per cycle.\n\nhyp-20261002-002-r2s1 ction is\nconsumed only when decode can advance without a load-use interlock,\ndmem hold, divider occupancy, or redirect. When the slot is empty or\nis being consumed, a ready instruction-memory response may refill it\non the same edge; advance the fetch PC by four exactly on that ref\n…[27427 of 30499 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9b763ee3d43b8d86]…\nr fitness from shortening fetch direction\nselection, with a small cycle cost possible from stale-counter and\nrecovery fallback guesses. Confidence is low because the new lookahead\npath and existing operand feedback may limit vendor Fmax. At unchanged\ncycles acceptance requires more than 98.133 MHz median Fmax; with changed\ncycles require median Fmax_Hz * 10 / measured_cycles above 228.40456.\nValidate the actual product, not just the cycle count or logic depth.\n\nTest consecutive branches, taken self-loops, loop exit/re-entry, JAL\nchains, correction onto a conditional branch, aliasing fetch/training\nindices, same-edge counter writes, reset and all hold causes. Assert\nthat each accepted instruction carries the prediction actually used\nto advance PC, holds preserve architectural operands, correction flushes\nall younger controls, and no wrong-path stores occur. Run unchanged\nformal/cosim and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO checks and median\nGowin timing. Prototype evidence does not replace implementation gates.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
