{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r9s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-001-r9s0\ntitle: \"Shorten RV32I arithmetic with parallel byte carry lookahead\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 218.36 iterations/sec at 93.817 MHz with\n  4,296,444 marker-bounded CoreMark cycles. Its recorded limiting path\n  crosses the ordinary ALU and EX-to-ID operand recurrence. Prior changes\n  to forwarding destinations, operation encodings, bitwise factoring and\n  stall enables have regressed; adding a recovery stage also costs cycles.\n  A local arithmetic implementation can shorten this recurrence while\n  retaining instruction throughput and every existing pipeline boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace only the combinational RV32I ADD and SUB arithmetic networks\n  in alu.sv with explicit four-byte parallel carry-lookahead arithmetic.\n  Keep distinct add and subtract candidates, the existing ALU output\n  selection and every other operation unchanged. Add no register or cycle.\n\n  For each 8-bit slice independently compute a 9-bit sum for carry-in\n  zero. Its carry-out is the group's generate bit; the reduction AND\n  of the slice's bitwise operand XOR is its propagate bit. Compute the\n  four actual byte carry inputs directly from the group generate and\n  propagate bits using expanded lookahead equations rather than chaining\n  selected byte carry outputs. In parallel compute each byte's carry-in\n  one candidate and select its low eight result bits with the resolved\n  carry input. For subtraction use a + ~b + 1 with the initial carry\n  equal to one; ADD uses a + b with initial carry zero. Drop overflow\n  beyond bit 31 exactly as RV32I requires. Use explicit widths, static\n  slice indices and generate blocks so Gowin sees short local carry\n  chains plus bounded lookahead and byte selection, not a new full