{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_59","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m refining the final proposal around a pipelined multiply lane that registers the full product and selects its result in MEM. Its partial prototype is close to the champion; predecoded multiply/divide class bits would also remove `alu_op` decoding from the busy and acceptance paths. This remains a low-confidence hypothesis—the measured partial prototype does not clear the acceptance bar."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r7s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r7s1',\n'title':'Pipeline full-width multiply completion with registered execution classes',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 218.36 iter/sec at 93.817 MHz and 4,296,444 marker-bounded CoreMark cycles. Registered multiplication previously gained 27.36 percent by breaking the multiply-to-ID recurrence. The r5s1 pipelined multiply attempt recovered independent issue slots but gained only 4.24 percent against its then-champion, below acceptance. It predates the champion's improved ID/EX payload/control squash policy. The r6s1 broad forwarding crossbar lost 6.40 percent, so expanding ordinary operand selection is poor evidence for another attempt. Target the multiplier's completion boundary and operation ownership instead.\\\\n\\\\nAn isolated full-product-to-MEM prototype on the current champion, with a decoded multiply bit but the old divide-class comparisons, produced Gowin placement-option Fmax values of 87.074, 93.422 and 93.422 MHz, with 3,673 LUTs and two DSPs. Its worst paths remain registered rs2 through EX into BHT updates. Other isolated structural probes exposed alu_op-to-multiply/divide-to-stage-acceptance paths. These are partial-prototype observations, not evidence that the final proposal passes the acceptance threshold.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Build a pipelined multiply completion lane whose register holds the full 64-bit architectural product, not the already selected 32-bit low/high result. Capture the product and high-half selection metadata on the same edge that an accepted EX multiply places its original instruction in EX/MEM. MEM owns the matching response, selects low or high product bits there, and uses that single selected architectural value for both completing-MEM forwarding and MEM/WB capture. Remove multiply from blocking EX occupancy and from the current EX bypass. This is one structural change to M-extension issue/completion ownership; preserve the ordinary RV32I pipeline, predictor, fetch topology and blocking divider.\\\\n\\\\nUnlike r5s1, move low/high product selection after the product register. Preserve the shared signed 33-by-33 multiplication and explicit 66-bit intermediate: sign-extend a only for MULH/MULHSU, sign-extend b only for MULH, and retain product[63:0]. MUL reads [31:0]; MULH/MULHU/MULHSU read [63:32]. Register the selection with the accepted transaction. Do not infer separate signed/unsigned multipliers or route a combinational product into younger operand capture. Upper intermediate bits 65:64 are redundant sign extension.\\\\n\\\\nRegister explicit is_multiply and is_divide bits in ID/EX from the validated decoder outputs. Use these bits consistently for EX occupancy, request launch, response routing, producer eligibility and dependency interlocks instead of comparing alu_op throughout the EX-to-ID control recurrence. Retain alu_op only where the arithmetic unit needs the exact operation. The class bits are part of instruction ownership and must clear with the entire control bundle on every bubble/squash and hold with operands during stalls. Derive them only from legal decoder results; preserve default-illegal decoding and every reserved-encoding trap. This predecoded scheduling is required in the final proposal; the partial full-product prototype did not include the divide bit.\\\\n\\\\nLaunch a multiply only on a valid, legal EX-to-EX/MEM advance with no older dmem hold. Allow a new request on the same edge the preceding MEM-owned response is consumed, giving independent consecutive multiplies one result per cycle. Request-ready can be \"'!response_valid || response_ready; response_ready is derived only from acceptance of the matching EX/MEM multiply, avoiding a combinational issue loop. Preserve response/product/selection until acceptance, clear validity after consumption without replacement, and invalidate all ownership on reset. Remove the redundant saved multiply EX payload and response-to-EX return path. The divider keeps its current saved payload, bounded execution, held response and blocking control.'\"\\\\n\\\\nExtend the existing load-use rule to valid EX multiplies. An immediately dependent consumer waits one bubble and then captures the actual MEM result; an independent successor advances without a multiply stall. Conservatively match both raw younger source fields to preserve exact RVFI operand values, including otherwise unused encoded fields. Include branch/JALR, effective-address, store-data and multiply/divide consumers, preserve x0 exclusion, and keep EX-over-MEM-over-write-first-RF priority. A dmem stall freezes EX/MEM and its product together and prohibits launch/replacement/consumption. A younger divider may occupy EX while an older multiply completes. Correctly predicted and mispredicted branches retain their current EX resolution and recovery latency. Squashed younger multiplies never launch; older multiplies are not canceled by younger recovery.\\\\n\\\\nCapture original instruction, PC/next-PC, destination and architectural operands in the existing pipeline registers. Preserve final trap-qualified writes, accepted load extraction, cleared side-effect controls in invalid bundles, the champion's complete reset and payload/control split, bus handshakes and single channel-0 ordered retirement. Under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, register the exact existing operation-specific stand-in instead of a real product, using the same pipelined request/response ownership and MEM selection latency. Apply formulas to all operands. Keep the ordinary ALU's M-extension inactive real outputs and its exact ALTOPS formulas unchanged.\\\\n\\\\nThe expectation is fewer unnecessary multiply issue bubbles and a shorter multiply/control recurrence. The earlier pipelined-MEM experiment measured 4,238,604 CoreMark cycles, but that count is a reference, not a measurement of this final implementation. At that count, acceptance requires median Fmax above approximately 96.812 MHz and fitness above 228.405 iter/sec. The partial prototype's 93.422 MHz does not meet this bar. Predict eight percent fitness improvement with low confidence from completing operation-class retiming and moving product selection behind the register. Risks are a persistent branch/BHT limit, extra 32 product registers, physical placement and product/metadata alignment under stalls. Measure the final median and marker-bounded cycles rather than extrapolating a win. Run unchanged formal and RVFI cosimulation gates, all real arithmetic suites, and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/marker/OOB validation. Preserve nret=1, exact top-level ports and every hard invariant; modify no evaluator, wrapper, benchmark or other core.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/mul_unit.sv','description':'Register the full product[63:0] and low/high selection on accepted requests, selecting the architectural 32-bit result from those registers. Support simultaneous consume/replacement and held responses. Preserve the shared signed 33-by-33 product, all RV32M corner cases, reset and exact ALTOPS formulas through identical transaction ownership.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add validated is_multiply and is_divide bits to ctrl_t and carry them through existing pipeline bundles. Preserve all architectural metadata and retirement width.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Predecode both M-extension operation classes from legal decoder outputs and register them with ID/EX controls. Preserve final operand capture, full reset, payload holds and full control/prediction clearing on bubbles.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Use registered class bits for scheduling. Launch multiplies on normal EX/MEM advance, remove multiply from busy and EX bypass, and remove saved multiply payload/return-to-EX completion. Export the registered product response to MEM while retaining existing blocking divider, trap handling and EX branch recovery.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Consume only the response owned by the accepted EX/MEM multiply. Select its registered low/high product as the common MEM-bypass/MEM-WB result. Hold ownership during older dmem stalls; retain memory outputs, load extraction, trap qualification and retirement suppression.'},\n{'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Extend the one-bubble result-unavailable dependency rule to valid EX multiplies, using the decoded class and nonzero destination matches on both raw sources. Retain redirect priority, load-use behavior, dmem precedence and divider drain/hold rules.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire MEM-owned multiply responses/acceptance and decoded operation classes, removing multiply response return through EX forwarding. Preserve exact nret=1 IO, memory handshakes and RVFI fields/order.'},\n{'file':'test/test_mul_unit.py','description':'Retain every real and ALTOPS arithmetic vector. Add consecutive accepted requests with alternating low/high operations, consume-and-replace, product/selection stability under response backpressure, request changes while blocked and reset. Check signed high-half and mixed signedness extremes.'},\n{'file':'test/test_id_stage.py','description':'Update packed control widths and verify both validated class bits, reserved encodings, complete squash clearing and operand/class retention during stalls without weakening current assertions.'},\n{'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'Adapt execute tests to MEM-owned multiply completion and decoded class fields. Retain divider, branch, trap and ordinary forwarding coverage; require no EX multiply bypass, no busy bubble for independent successors, and no launch under invalid/squashed/dmem-held conditions.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Update packed control layouts and multiplier ownership expectations. Check independent and back-to-back multiply issue, exactly one bubble for immediate dependent consumers, competing writers, high-half results, x0, memory stalls, older multiply completion during younger divide/recovery and reset. Require exact RVFI operands/results/PC/order and no repeated or wrong-path memory effects.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=92))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['id']=='hyp-20261002-002-r7s1' and h['category']=='structural'\nprint('Final hypothesis written and schema validated:',p)\nprint('Target acceptance Fmax at reference cycles:',218.36*1.046*4238604/1e7)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
