{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r5s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-003-r5s2\ntitle: Local prediction squash clears control while freeing payload registers\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: 'The current predictor already avoids recurring direct-control bubbles,\n\n  but every EX misprediction still drives a whole-ID/EX zeroing mux. Its\n\n  flush and invalid-fetch handling clear PC, instruction, target, operands,\n\n  ALU inputs and destination metadata as well as valid and control bits.\n\n  Prediction recovery needs to invalidate the instruction and clear every\n\n  side-effect control; clearing the inactive payload adds substantial\n\n  fanout and input muxing to a timing-sensitive recovery path. Prior BHT\n\n  banking changed CoreMark cycles by only 0.29 percent. Reduce the physical\n\n  cost of existing predictor recovery instead of adding table complexity.'\nhypothesis: 'Localize prediction recovery in ID/EX: keep the exact BHT/JAL guesses,\n\n  direction-XOR EX correction, resolved targets and pipeline boundaries,\n\n  but remove flush and fetch-validity gating from the data payload flops.\n\n  Split the current whole-struct ID/EX assignment into payload update and\n\n  control/valid/prediction update logic, retaining a single owner for each register bit.\n\n\n  On reset clear every field exactly as today. Outside reset, update PC,\n\n  instruction, immediate, rs1/rs2 values and addresses, alu_a/alu_b, rd and direct_target\n  whenever !stall, using their existing\n\n  combinational sources. These data fields must not depend on flush or\n\n  in.valid and may contain a wrong-path or unavailable fetch in a bubble.\n\n  When stall is asserted, hold these payload fields even if flush requests\n\n  an injected bubble. Independently, flush has priority over stall and\n\n  clears the entire ctrl_t plus valid and predicted_taken. Also clear ctrl_t, valid and predicted_taken\n  when\n\n  !stall && !in.valid. Otherwise an unstalled valid input captures the\n\n  existing decoded control bundle and predicted_taken, and sets valid; a\n\n  stall without flush holds control, valid and predicted_taken. This is precisely th\n…[2924 of 5996 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a507977b43515e93]…\nsources on !stall and hold on stall, independent of\n\n    flush and in.valid. Flush or an unstalled invalid fetch clears all ctrl_t\n\n    bits, valid and predicted_taken. An unstalled valid input captures the\n\n    existing ctrl_t and predicted_taken and sets valid; otherwise those three\n\n    hold. Keep one owner per bit and all architectural operand muxes intact.'\n- file: test/test_pipeline.py\n  description: 'Retain all invariant tests and add checks that every prediction squash\n\n    and imem bubble clears valid, predicted_taken and the entire control bundle even when\n\n    payload bits are retained or capture unavailable/wrong-path data.\n\n    Exercise mispredicted branches followed by stores, loads, MUL/DIV,\n\n    reserved encodings and JALR; correction with imem/load-use interlocks;\n\n    and branches/arithmetic held behind older dmem stalls. Verify payload\n\n    hold during stalls, no invalid arithmetic launch/forwarding/training/\n\n    memory/retirement, exact RVFI PC/next-PC sequence, order and trap behavior.'\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
