{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')\"'!='\"'predictor': continue\n print('\\\\nID',d['id'],'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),'Fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'))\n print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n if d.get('implementation_notes'): print('NOTES',d['implementation_notes'][:5000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 fitness 13.02 cycles 4197517 Fmax 5.467\nAdd a 64-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer with an integrated\ntwo-bit saturating direction counter. Index with fetch PC[7:2]; retain\nthe full PC[31:8] tag, a word-aligned 30-bit target, a conditional versus\nunconditional type bit, and a valid bit. A matching unconditional entry\npredicts its cached target; a conditional entry predicts that target\nwhen the counter MSB is set. A miss or not-taken prediction chooses pc+4.\nInclude JAL and JALR in the same predictor, using the last resolved\ntarget for JALR, without adding a return stack or global history.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC alongside each accepted instruction\nthrough IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX compute the architectural next PC using\nthe existing forwarded operands, branch comparison, JALR bit-zero\nclearing, and misalignment/trap rules. Redirect and flush only when a\nvalid instruction advances out of EX and its actual next PC differs\nfrom its saved prediction. Check this equality for every instruction,\nincluding non-control and trapped instructions, so stale entries cannot\nsilently skip architectural instructions. RVFI pc_wdata always reports\nthe resolved next PC, never the prediction. Correctly predicted taken\ncontrol flow captures the target instruction normally without a bubble.\n\nTrain once per valid EX advance, never during dmem holds or bubbles.\nAllocate only decoded legal control-flow instructions with aligned\ntargets; initialize conditional counters to weakly taken or weakly\nnot-taken according to the resolved direction, and saturate on later\ntag-matching updates. Unconditional entries always predict taken and\nupdate their target on each resolution. Invalidate a matching stale\nentry when a non-control instruction or trapping control flow resolves\nthere. Clear valid bits on reset; other table fields need not reset and\nmust be ignored when invalid. Use a compact asynchronous-read,\nsynchronous-write table rather than a fully associative search.\n\nPreserve the existing bus and load-us\n…[16543 of 19615 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e8a4130ecedea215]…\nhe expected\nmedian is 93.817 MHz if option 2 agrees, giving 218.360 iter/sec for the\nchampion's unchanged 4,296,444 measured cycles, about 5.21 percent above\n207.55 and above the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold. Area is 3,675 LUTs\nand 1,090 FFs versus 3,790 and 1,090, with unchanged memories and two DSPs.\nStalled canonical CoreMark completes with all five expected CRCs, the\nvalidation literal, correct MMIO brackets and no OOB access. A closely\nrelated version clearing only ctrl_t and valid passed the existing\npipeline cocotb suite, stalled selftest and all three random cosimulation\nprograms, plus targeted branch/JALR, PC, illegal, order and liveness formal\nchecks; that version reached only 93.050 MHz median. Preserve clearing\npredicted_taken in the proposed final version. Final median timing and\ncorrectness validation must use this exact RTL, not the earlier variant.\nRe-run the unchanged formal/cosimulation gates and directed squash/hold\nchecks; this proposal changes only prediction-recovery state handling.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
