{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_52", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -B - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema,shutil\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r9s2', 'title':'Post accepted branch training through a one-cycle update register', 'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The 218.36 iter/sec champion already predicts direct branches and JAL. Reference profiling finds only 13,735 returns in the timed region, limiting perfect return prediction to about 0.32 percent cycle savings. Prior folded indexing saved only 0.29 percent of cycles; agree and registered-lookup designs reduced Fmax. Recent timing paths repeatedly run from EX branch operands through the predictor counter update, with 37-38 cells. The current counter update consumes the live combinational branch outcome. Registering the accepted training transaction can break this timing path while preserving the working predictor's size, hysteresis, index and fetch latency.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add one pipeline register for conditional-branch training inside if_stage, between the existing EX acceptance interface and the unchanged BHT update logic. Save train_valid_q, the seven-bit branch_index, and branch_taken. On every non-reset edge set train_valid_q to the current branch_update; capture index/outcome when branch_update is true. Reset train_valid_q to zero and retain the BHT's current reset to weakly not taken. Index/outcome payload need not reset because an invalid transaction never writes the table.\n\nAt the same edge, apply the previously registered transaction to the existing 128-entry two-bit table. Compute saturation from the LIVE counter at train_index_q on the application cycle, incrementing for train_taken_q and decrementing otherwise. Do not capture a precomputed next counter: consecutive branches or aliases at the same index must see the preceding applied write and preserve update order. This one-entry pipeline consumes an older transaction and captures a new one every cycle, so it needs no full/ready signal and supports one accepted branch per cycle without dropping or repeating updates.\n\nThe register captures exactly the existing valid, legal, aligned EX-to-EX/MEM branch acceptance event. Keep ex_stage's branch_update/index/outcome generation and all pipeline stages unchanged. A transaction that was already accepted must drain exactly once even if the following cycle has an imem stall, dmem hold, load-use interlock, arithmetic wait or EX correction. These younger conditions must not hold or replay train_valid_q. Bubbles capture an invalid transaction; reset discards any queued event and resets the table. The table may update during a later pipeline hold because its transaction belongs to an already accepted instruction.\n\nFetch keeps the existing combinational lookup at PC[8:2], instruction qualification, aligned direct target, always-taken direct JAL, sequential JALR, and completed-address selection. No table banking, folded index, alternative encoding, outcome-to-fetch bypass or registered fetch lookup. The only prediction-policy change is one cycle later learning. Same-edge table reads observe the pre-write state under the existing nonblocking semantics. Fetch/ID must continue carrying the exact predicted_taken used when the instruction is accepted; ordinary EX direction-XOR recovery makes delayed guesses architecturally harmless.\n\nThis splits registered EX operands to comparator to training register from registered training to counter read/saturation/write, removing the long comparator prefix and its fanout from table-write timing. Keep correction in EX with its existing latency, preserve every payload/control hold and squash rule, top-level nret=1 ports, architectural operands and resolved RVFI PC, illegal and misalignment traps, link write suppression, real M arithmetic, memory handshakes and consecutive retirement order. No benchmark, contract, wrapper, tool or other-core change.\n\nExpect approximately six percent higher fitness if decoupling predictor training raises Fmax beyond the 4.6 percent acceptance margin, with a small warmup/aliasing cycle cost possible from delayed learning. Confidence is low because the unchanged ALU/forwarding and branch-recovery cones may still limit timing. Require measured median Fmax_Hz times 10 divided by marker-bounded CoreMark cycles above 228.40456 iter/sec; at unchanged cycles this needs more than 98.133 MHz. Test same-index consecutive updates, alternating outcomes, saturation, aliasing, reset with a pending event, and a pending write coincident with every hold/correction cause. Verify exactly one applied write per accepted branch and the delayed predictor's exact predictions, then unchanged full formal/cosim gates and all canonical CRC/UART/MMIO checks.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Register existing accepted branch_update/index/outcome as a one-cycle training transaction. Apply the prior transaction once per cycle using live counter saturation; drain independently of pipeline holds and clear pending validity on reset. Preserve the BHT implementation, index and combinational fetch prediction.'},\n {'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'Add a focused fetch/predictor cocotb suite with a cycle-accurate one-cycle delayed training model. Cover same-index and aliased back-to-back transactions, saturation, alternating outcomes, no-update bubbles, old-data fetch/write behavior, reset of pending transactions and writes during fetch holds/corrections.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Verify queued updates correspond exactly once and in order to accepted legal aligned branches, including independent bus stalls, load-use holds, M occupancy and recovery. Preserve exact RVFI PC/result/trap/order and wrong-path-store assertions and check predictions against the delayed-training model.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=80));jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nback=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/6b356b5e/bbd81d95/branch_recovery_backup')\nfor name in ['core_pkg.sv','id_stage.sv','ex_stage.sv','if_stage.sv']:\n shutil.copyfile(back/name,Path('cores/bench/rtl')/name)\nprint('Wrote valid posted-training hypothesis and restored all temporary RTL changes.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_52", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -B - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema,shutil\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-003-r9s2', 'title':'Post accepted branch training through a one-cycle update register', 'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The 218.36 iter/sec champion already predicts direct branches and JAL. Reference profiling finds only 13,735 returns in the timed region, limiting perfect return prediction to about 0.32 percent cycle savings. Prior folded indexing saved only 0.29 percent of cycles; agree and registered-lookup designs reduced Fmax. Recent timing paths repeatedly run from EX branch operands through the predictor counter update, with 37-38 cells. The current counter update consumes the live combinational branch outcome. Registering the accepted training transaction can break this timing path while preserving the working predictor's size, hysteresis, index and fetch latency.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add one pipeline register for conditional-branch training inside if_stage, between the existing EX acceptance interface and the unchanged BHT update logic. Save train_valid_q, the seven-bit branch_index, and branch_taken. On every non-reset edge set train_valid_q to the current branch_update; capture index/outcome when branch_update is true. Reset train_valid_q to zero and retain the BHT's current reset to weakly not taken. Index/outcome payload need not reset because an invalid transaction never writes the table.\n\nAt the same edge, apply the previously registered transaction to the existing 128-entry two-bit table. Compute saturation from the LIVE counter at train_index_q on the application cycle, incrementing for train_taken_q and decrementing otherwise. Do not capture a precomputed next counter: consecutive branches or aliases at the same index must see the preceding applied write and preserve update order. This one-entry pipeline consumes an older transaction and captures a new one every cycle, so it needs no full/ready signal and supports one accepted branch per cycle without dropping or repeating updates.\n\nThe register captures exactly the existing valid, legal, aligned EX-to-EX/MEM branch acceptance event. Keep ex_stage's branch_update/index/outcome generation and all pipeline stages unchanged. A transaction that was already accepted must drain exactly once even if the following cycle has an imem stall, dmem hold, load-use interlock, arithmetic wait or EX correction. These younger conditions must not hold or replay train_valid_q. Bubbles capture an invalid transaction; reset discards any queued event and resets the table. The table may update during a later pipeline hold because its transaction belongs to an already accepted instruction.\n\nFetch keeps the existing combinational lookup at PC[8:2], instruction qualification, aligned direct target, always-taken direct JAL, sequential JALR, and completed-address selection. No table banking, folded index, alternative encoding, outcome-to-fetch bypass or registered fetch lookup. The only prediction-policy change is one cycle later learning. Same-edge table reads observe the pre-write state under the existing nonblocking semantics. Fetch/ID must continue carrying the exact predicted_taken used when the instruction is accepted; ordinary EX direction-XOR recovery makes delayed guesses architecturally harmless.\n\nThis splits registered EX operands to comparator to training register from registered training to counter read/saturation/write, removing the long comparator prefix and its fanout from table-write timing. Keep correction in EX with its existing latency, preserve every payload/control hold and squash rule, top-level nret=1 ports, architectural operands and resolved RVFI PC, illegal and misalignment traps, link write suppression, real M arithmetic, memory handshakes and consecutive retirement order. No benchmark, contract, wrapper, tool or other-core change.\n\nExpect approximately six percent higher fitness if decoupling predictor training raises Fmax beyond the 4.6 percent acceptance margin, with a small warmup/aliasing cycle cost possible from delayed learning. Confidence is low because the unchanged ALU/forwarding and branch-recovery cones may still limit timing. Require measured median Fmax_Hz times 10 divided by marker-bounded CoreMark cycles above 228.40456 iter/sec; at unchanged cycles this needs more than 98.133 MHz. Test same-index consecutive updates, alternating outcomes, saturation, aliasing, reset with a pending event, and a pending write coincident with every hold/correction cause. Verify exactly one applied write per accepted branch and the delayed predictor's exact predictions, then unchanged full formal/cosim gates and all canonical CRC/UART/MMIO checks.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Register existing accepted branch_update/index/outcome as a one-cycle training transaction. Apply the prior transaction once per cycle using live counter saturation; drain independently of pipeline holds and clear pending validity on reset. Preserve the BHT implementation, index and combinational fetch prediction.'},\n {'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'Add a focused fetch/predictor cocotb suite with a cycle-accurate one-cycle delayed training model. Cover same-index and aliased back-to-back transactions, saturation, alternating outcomes, no-update bubbles, old-data fetch/write behavior, reset of pending transactions and writes during fetch holds/corrections.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Verify queued updates correspond exactly once and in order to accepted legal aligned branches, including independent bus stalls, load-use holds, M occupancy and recovery. Preserve exact RVFI PC/result/trap/order and wrong-path-store assertions and check predictions against the delayed-training model.'}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=80));jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nback=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/6b356b5e/bbd81d95/branch_recovery_backup')\nfor name in ['core_pkg.sv','id_stage.sv','ex_stage.sv','if_stage.sv']:\n shutil.copyfile(back/name,Path('cores/bench/rtl')/name)\nprint('Wrote valid posted-training hypothesis and restored all temporary RTL changes.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Wrote valid posted-training hypothesis and restored all temporary RTL changes.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
