{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +735.07%): Moving combinational DIV/REM (`/` and `%`) out of the shared ALU into a registered iterative divider removes their timing cost from ordinary instructions, letting Fmax gains outweigh CoreMark's added divide stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (regression, +7.45%): Registering elastic IF/ID removes redirect-to-decode feedback, but the added taken-branch refill cycle raised CoreMark cycles 6.45%, limiting a 14.39% Fmax gain to 7.45% fitness.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 (improvement, +13.63%): Instruction-qualified BHT/JAL prediction removes recurring CoreMark control bubbles, while saved direct targets enable direction-XOR recovery and full ID/EX control clearing keeps correction out of decode/register-file read paths.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r3s0 (regression, +15.76%): Opcode-selected sign-extension bits on one signed 33-by-33 product and a shared high-half case arm cut Gowin DSP usage from six to two and shorten the multiplier/result-mux path without changing CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r3s1 (improvement, +41.70%): Capturing bypass-resolved rs1/rs2 and final alu_a/alu_b in ID/EX removes the shared register-file/forwarding timing prefix from both multiplier and branch-recovery paths without adding a pipeline stage.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r3s2 (regression, +9.23%): Resetting validity bits instead of two-bit BHT payloads makes banked asynchronous-read arrays LUT-RAM eligible without adding fetch latency, but the unchanged ALU/forwarding critical path limits gains compared with operand-path retiming.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r4s0 (regression, +14.05%): A balanced tree of two-bit equality/less-than leaves fits four-input LUTs and shortens the EX branch comparator carry-chain path without changing CoreMark cycles, shifting the timing limit to ALU-result-to-ID forwarding.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r4s1 (improvement, +27.36%): Routing MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU through a single registered response breaks the comb\n…[1651 of 4723 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b1d5731bc2222ff9]…\n MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r6s2 (regression, -6.04%): One-bit agree writes eliminate the saturating-counter training lookup and reduce predictor area but leave EX-to-ID operand feedback critical; Fmax fell from 93.817 to 88.547 MHz while CoreMark cycles rose only 0.45%, showing that simpler training does not guarantee faster timing.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r7s2 (regression, -7.05%): With PC redirect and ID control flush already overriding stalls, removing redirect from stall_if/stall_id preserves CoreMark cycles but leaves the 38-cell EX rs2-to-ID ALU-input D path through redirect_Z and rs2_value, so clock-enable simplification alone can regress Fmax.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261002-003-r9s2 (regression, -12.68%): Registering BHT training with four 32-row validity-reset LUT-RAM banks shifts the timing limit to ID/EX ALU control through EX forwarding into ID operands, so area savings alone cannot prevent Fmax falling from 93.817 to 81.919 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
