{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n e=json.loads(l)\n if e.get('category')=='micro_opt' and any('-r'+str(i)+'s' in e['id'] for i in range(7,15)):\n  print(e['id'],e['title'])\n  h=e.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES:',e.get('implementation_notes','')[-4500:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-001-r7s0 Align M operation codes to shorten EX class selection\nRecode only the eight internal M-extension ALU tags into a naturally aligned block, retaining all RV32I tags 0..10 and the five-bit tag width:\n  MUL=16, MULH=17, MULHSU=18, MULHU=19,\n  DIV=20, DIVU=21, REM=22, REMU=23.\nThese values are {2'b10, instruction_funct3} for the eight validated RV32M instructions. Multiply is exactly alu_op[4:2]==3'b100 and division/remainder is exactly alu_op[4:2]==3'b101. Replace the two EX four-way full-tag classifiers with those bounded three-bit comparisons. Preserve explicit whole-family matching: bit 4 alone must not classify undefined tags 24..31 as M operations.\n\nKeep decoder opcode/funct validation and default-illegal behavior unchanged; its existing symbolic ALU constants now produce the new tags. Keep every other consumer symbolic, including ordinary ALU dispatch, exact ALTOPS formulas, multiplier signedness/high-half selection and divider operation selection. The natural low bits also expose DIV/REM signedness and remainder selection to synthesis through the existing constant comparisons, without changing their arithmetic or request/response protocols. Do not introduce new class registers, alter instruction encodings, widen ctrl_t, change pipeline boundaries, or combine this with result-crossbar, shifter, comparator or prediction changes.\n\nThe intended gain is fewer inputs and less decoding/routing on multiply/divide occupancy, request launch, completion selection and bypass control. Operand data, trap-adjusted enables, launch-once ownership, response holds, saved destinations, x0 exclusion and EX-over-MEM-over-RF forwarding priority retain their current behavior. Numeric tag values are internal; all actual RV32IM results, instruction latencies, stalled bus acceptance and RVFI records must remain identical.\n\nWith unchanged canonical CoreMark cycles, acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax above approximately 98.133 MHz and fitness above 228.405 iter/sec. This is a low-confidence timing hypothesis: smal\n…[30493 of 33565 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f078dd4707e19951]…\nout masks and masked-OR routing may\ninstead regress timing; competing branch and accepted-load paths may\nlimit the gain. This proposal has not established a timing improvement.\nAt 4,296,444 cycles the 4.6 percent acceptance margin requires fitness\nabove 228.405 iter/sec, approximately 98.14 MHz median Gowin Fmax.\n\nVerify all one-hot bits against the existing operation encoding, default\nzero for unsupported op values, and compare both ALU modes for arbitrary\noperands in real and ALTOPS builds. Check selector capture/hold/squash\nwith its owning instruction and back-to-back mixed operations using EX,\naccepted MEM and RF operands under independent bus stalls. Retain every\nexisting assertion. Require unchanged formal and stalled ISS/RVFI cosim,\ncanonical CoreMark CRCs and validation literal, bounded memory and MMIO\ntiming brackets. Inspect synthesis to confirm encoded-op decoding is\nabsent from the real core ALU result cone, then measure all three Gowin\nplacement options and actual marker-bounded cycles.\n\nNOTES: \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
