{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'false|source.*use|uses_rs|unused|load.use|hazard' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-600\"", "aggregated_output": "2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share a width-tapered combinational divider across RV32M operations\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline's worst timed path crosses forwarding and the ALU, reaching\\n199.837 ns through 156 cells at a median Fmax of 5.442 MHz. alu.sv expresses\\nDIV, DIVU, REM and REMU as separate signed/unsigned division and remainder\\noperators. Their combinational implementation is the strongest suspected\\nsource of the deep arithmetic path and substantial LUT usage. A smaller,\\nexplicitly shared divider can improve the timed circuit even when Core\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Move division out of the single-cycle execute path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The only recorded bench experiment is the baseline: 5.442 MHz with a\\n199.837 ns worst path spanning forwarding, EX, and the combinational ALU\\nthrough 156 cells. There are no previous structural attempts or recorded\\nlessons. The ALU implements signed and unsigned divide and remainder with\\ncombinational / and % operators. These unrolled arithmetic networks remain\\nin the timed result-selection cone even when an ordinary RV32I instruction\\nexecutes. Moving \n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged target predictor eliminates recurrent control-flow bubbles\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches pc+4 and redirects in EX for every taken\\nconditional branch, JAL, or JALR. Since IF/ID is combinational rather\\nthan a separate register, each redirect discards one younger instruction\\nand inserts a bubble. CoreMark repeatedly executes loops and stable\\ndirect jumps, so many of these bubbles should be pred