{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r10s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r10s1',\n 'title':'Register carry preparation in ID and resolve shared arithmetic in EX',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion achieves 218.36 iterations/sec at 93.817 MHz with\n4,296,444 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Recent structural attempts leave\nEX arithmetic and result forwarding into ID/EX as a recurring timing\nlimit. r8s1 moved recovery into MEM but added 67,904 cycles and scored\n217.64; r9s1 buffered fetch and scored 225.27, below the 4.6 percent\nacceptance threshold. Isolated fall-through queue experiments in this\nproposal turn reduced cycles but lost enough vendor Fmax to regress.\nThe next promising structural target is the arithmetic register boundary,\nwith unchanged instruction latency, rather than another frontend buffer.\n''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Retiming one shared add/subtract/compare datapath across the existing\nID/EX register is the entire architectural hypothesis. Keep fetch,\nprediction, stage count, blocking multiplier/divider and dependency\ninterlocks as in the champion. ID still resolves architectural operands\nwith EX-over-MEM-over-write-first-RF priority and selects final ALU inputs.\nAlongside the existing registered operands, capture bitwise carry\npreparation rather than doing a multi-bit addition before the register.\n\nFor legal ADD, ADDI, address generation, AUIPC and JALR use a=alu_a,\nb=alu_b and s=0. SUB, SLT/SLTI, SLTU/SLTIU and conditional branches use\ns=1. A conditional branch's selected inputs are its architectural rs1\nand rs2. Form p = a XOR b XOR {32{s}} and g = a AND (b XOR {32{s}}),\nand register p, g and s together with the owning instruction. These\nare bit-local operations with no carry chain in ID. Retain the original\nalu_a/alu_b and architectural source values for other ALU operations,\nRV32M units, store data, signs and exact RVFI operands. The preparation\nregisters must follow the existing payload reset, advance and stall-hold\npolicy, with validity and side effects governed by the original controls.\n\nEX consumes only these registered p/g/s values to produce the arithmetic\nsum, carry-out and comparison results. Use an explicit balanced prefix\ncarry network over registered p/g, with bounded fanout and a topology\nchosen for LUT4s; do not reintroduce a serial 32-bit ripple recurrence.\nThe associative combine is (Ghi OR Phi*Glo, Phi*Plo); each carry is the\nprefix generate OR prefix propagate*s. Sum bits are p XOR carry-in.\nUnsigned less-than for subtraction is NOT final carry-out. Signed\nless-than uses operand signs when they differ, otherwise unsigned\nless-than. For a subtract-prepared branch, all p bits being one means\nequality; use that and the same signed/unsigned carry comparison for\nall six conditional branches. Route JALR target addition through this\nsame prepared arithmetic result, then clear bit zero and retain the\nexisting bit-one alignment fault. Keep PC+4 and saved direct targets\non their existing paths.\n\nThe shared combinational alu module must retain its ordinary default\ninterface behavior for existing unit tests. A constant parameter or\nseparate nonarithmetic execution module may disable ADD/SUB/SLT/SLTU\ninside the core's ALU instance, whose results now come from the prepared\nprefix lane. Synthesis must actually remove those duplicate full-width\narithmetic paths. Retain bitwise operations and barrel shifts at their\ncurrent latency, and preserve every real M-extension and ALTOPS formula.\nDo not move M arithmetic into the new carry lane or change its handshakes.\n\nThe structural benefit is moving initial bit carry preparation before\nID/EX while avoiding a long arithmetic cone after forwarding in ID.\nA tested preliminary variant registered four eight-bit carry-select\nsums, but its ordinary ID addition made timing substantially worse:\n61.752 MHz on placement option zero despite correct CoreMark at exactly\n4,296,444 cycles. Do not implement that variant, nor compute sum1 by\nincrementing a captured-candidate sum0 before ID/EX. This final bitwise\npreparation differs from it and must be evaluated on its own merits.\nUse a hierarchical or sparse balanced prefix if a dense prefix creates\nexcessive routing; this is a mapping choice within the single retimed\narithmetic structure, not another architectural hypothesis.\n\nNo instruction gains a stage or an execution bubble. Keep the original\nEX forwarding acceptance, final trap qualification, branch correction\nlatency, once-per-accepted branch training, dmem precedence, load-use\nbubble, M launch-once and held response rules. Invalid bundles cannot\nforward, redirect, launch arithmetic, access memory or retire. Preserve\nfull reset, payload/control squash split, decoder default-illegal\nvalidation, EBREAK-only SYSTEM legality, aligned memory masks, every\nexisting trap and monotonically consecutive channel-zero RVFI orders.\nMaintain nret=1 and all top-level ports and benchmark/evaluator contracts.\n\nPredict eight percent fitness improvement with low confidence, from\nshorter ordinary arithmetic/branch feedback at unchanged CoreMark\ncycles. This is a prediction, not an accepted measured result. At\n4,296,444 cycles, acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax above 98.133 MHz\nand fitness above 228.405 iterations/sec. Register and prefix routing\ncosts, shifter/result-selection delay, multiplier control and the extra\nbitwise preparation after forwarding can erase the gain. Require actual\nmedian vendor timing and marker-bounded cycles; area or a shorter logic\npath alone is insufficient. Run unchanged formal, real arithmetic,\nRVFI cosimulation and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/OOB/marker gates.\n''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Extend id_ex_t with registered 32-bit arithmetic propagate and generate\nvectors and subtraction carry-in. Preserve original operands, controls,\nPC/prediction metadata and all architectural bundle meanings. This adds\ninternal arithmetic preparation, with no top-level IO or nret change.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Form bit-local p/g preparation from the fully forwarded final ALU\ninputs, complementing b and choosing carry-in for SUB/SLT/SLTU and\nconditional branches. Capture p/g/s at the same instruction-admission\nedge as the original operands, with complete reset and identical payload\nstall holds. Perform no multi-bit add, increment or carry propagation in\nID. Keep exact captured RVFI operands and original control squash policy.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Resolve the registered carry preparation with a balanced LUT4-oriented\nprefix structure, sharing the resulting arithmetic among ADD/SUB,\nsigned/unsigned SLT, conditional comparisons and JALR address formation.\nUse sign mismatch for signed comparison and subtract-propagate reduction\nfor branch equality. Select the prefix result into existing EX/MEM and\nEX bypass without adding a stage or interlock. Preserve original trap,\nredirect, training, launch-once, held arithmetic response and bus rules.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Keep the default combinational ALU behavior and unit-test interface.\nProvide a constant specialization used by EX that excludes duplicate\nADD/SUB/SLT/SLTU implementations, since their architectural results come\nfrom registered carry preparation. Preserve all other operations, the\nexact ALTOPS stand-ins and inactive real M outputs; ensure synthesis\ncannot retain another full-width arithmetic path in the shared result.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'''Update packed ID/EX layouts and operand stimulus to supply correctly\nprepared p/g/s with each instruction. Retain all existing assertions.\nCheck carry crossing every prefix boundary, overflow and signedness,\nall branch conditions, JALR low-bit clearing/alignment traps and valid\nbubbles, without substituting weaker arithmetic or trap expectations.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Update packed bundle layouts and check preparation registers follow\npayload reset, advance and hold behavior. Exercise arithmetic and\nbranch preparation from EX, accepted load/MEM and WB/RF sources;\ninclude x0, immediate/PC selection, alternating add/subtract, carry\nboundaries, signed extremes, and dependent branches/JALR under independent\nbus stalls. Preserve every existing exact RVFI, retirement, memory-effect,\narithmetic, decoder and progression check. Confirm unchanged canonical\nCoreMark cycles and evaluate median Gowin Fmax after implementation.\n'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised structural hypothesis valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r10s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r10s1',\n 'title':'Register carry preparation in ID and resolve shared arithmetic in EX',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion achieves 218.36 iterations/sec at 93.817 MHz with\n4,296,444 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Recent structural attempts leave\nEX arithmetic and result forwarding into ID/EX as a recurring timing\nlimit. r8s1 moved recovery into MEM but added 67,904 cycles and scored\n217.64; r9s1 buffered fetch and scored 225.27, below the 4.6 percent\nacceptance threshold. Isolated fall-through queue experiments in this\nproposal turn reduced cycles but lost enough vendor Fmax to regress.\nThe next promising structural target is the arithmetic register boundary,\nwith unchanged instruction latency, rather than another frontend buffer.\n''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Retiming one shared add/subtract/compare datapath across the existing\nID/EX register is the entire architectural hypothesis. Keep fetch,\nprediction, stage count, blocking multiplier/divider and dependency\ninterlocks as in the champion. ID still resolves architectural operands\nwith EX-over-MEM-over-write-first-RF priority and selects final ALU inputs.\nAlongside the existing registered operands, capture bitwise carry\npreparation rather than doing a multi-bit addition before the register.\n\nFor legal ADD, ADDI, address generation, AUIPC and JALR use a=alu_a,\nb=alu_b and s=0. SUB, SLT/SLTI, SLTU/SLTIU and conditional branches use\ns=1. A conditional branch's selected inputs are its architectural rs1\nand rs2. Form p = a XOR b XOR {32{s}} and g = a AND (b XOR {32{s}}),\nand register p, g and s together with the owning instruction. These\nare bit-local operations with no carry chain in ID. Retain the origi