{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')=='structural':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('\\\\nID',d['id'], d.get('title'), '\\\\nRESULT',d.get('outcome'),d.get('fitness'),d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'))\n  print('PATH',d.get('critical_path'))\n  print('HYPOTHESIS', h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 Move division out of the single-cycle execute path \nRESULT improvement 101.21 45.46 4491817\nPATH {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_9_s1', 'slack_ns': -17.306, 'arrival_ns': 27.6, 'logic_cells': 47, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']}\nHYPOTHESIS Replace all four combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU paths with one shared,\nblocking, iterative divider connected to EX. Keep RV32I execution and\nmultiplication on their existing single-cycle paths. The divider captures\nthe fully forwarded operands and operation exactly once, converts signed\noperands to unsigned magnitudes, and performs one radix-2 restoring\ndivision step per cycle using a registered partial remainder, quotient,\ndivisor, and a bounded counter. Share the magnitude datapath between signed\nand unsigned operations and between quotient and remainder; register sign\ncorrection before presenting the result. A normal division takes about\n34-35 cycles including preparation and completion. Explicitly implement\ndivide-by-zero and signed INT_MIN/-1 semantics with bounded early finish.\n\nWhile the divider runs, hold the instruction in ID/EX and stop fetch/decode,\nbut let older EX/MEM and MEM/WB instructions drain. Insert invalid bubbles\ninto EX/MEM rather than freezing an already completed memory request or\nreplaying an older retirement. Clear reg_write, mem_read, mem_write, and\nredirect controls in these bubbles as well as valid, because the current\nmemory and forwarding logic consumes control bits without validity gates.\nAn older dmem stall still holds EX/MEM and\ntakes precedence over divider issue and result transfer. Latch forwarded\noperands at accepted launch so draining WB cannot change an active divide.\nRetain an unconsumed registered result until EX/MEM can accept it, transfer\nit exactly once with the original instruction's RVFI metadata, then resume\nthe pipeline. Preserve the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding priority;\nthe completed divide result \n…[47760 of 50832 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:dbddfe50920b1fcf]…\netic, access memory or retire. Preserve\nfull reset, payload/control squash split, decoder default-illegal\nvalidation, EBREAK-only SYSTEM legality, aligned memory masks, every\nexisting trap and monotonically consecutive channel-zero RVFI orders.\nMaintain nret=1 and all top-level ports and benchmark/evaluator contracts.\n\nPredict eight percent fitness improvement with low confidence, from\nshorter ordinary arithmetic/branch feedback at unchanged CoreMark\ncycles. This is a prediction, not an accepted measured result. At\n4,296,444 cycles, acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax above 98.133 MHz\nand fitness above 228.405 iterations/sec. Register and prefix routing\ncosts, shifter/result-selection delay, multiplier control and the extra\nbitwise preparation after forwarding can erase the gain. Require actual\nmedian vendor timing and marker-bounded cycles; area or a shorter logic\npath alone is insufficient. Run unchanged formal, real arithmetic,\nRVFI cosimulation and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/OOB/marker gates.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
