{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"agent_message","text":"The fetch-buffer variants reduced cycles, but their timing results stayed well below the required Fmax. I’m replacing that proposal with a different structural hypothesis: register bypass candidates and source tags separately, then select operands in EX. This targets the result-to-operand feedback path that remained critical in the probes."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_46", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r11s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261003-002-r11s1\ntitle: \\\"Separate operand-candidate registers from bypass-source selection\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 218.36 iter/sec at 93.817 MHz and 4,296,444\n  marker-bounded CoreMark cycles. The recurring timing limit is EX/MEM\n  result or completion qualification through the ID forwarding network\n  into younger registered operands. r3s1 successfully registered final\n  operands, and r4s1 registered multiply responses, but r6s1's unregistered\n  six-candidate crossbar and r10s1's arithmetic preparation regressed.\n  More fetch buffering does not address this feedback: scratch five-slot\n  registered-head queues passed canonical CoreMark at 4,061,023 cycles,\n  but tested Gowin placement-option-zero Fmax values were only 77.818 to\n  82.696 MHz. Even actual LUT-RAM extraction and 1,196 registers left\n  operand/control feedback critical. Do not implement those queue probes.\n  The next structural opportunity is to register candidate values and\n  dependency decisions separately, removing acceptance-qualified wide\n  operand selection from the result-to-register path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace ID's resolved-operand register boundary with a registered\n  operand-candidate bank and registered source tags. This is one structural\n  retiming of operand resolution, not a new frontend or arithmetic design.\n  Preserve the champion's live fetch, predictor, stage count, ordinary\n  instruction latency, load-use bubble, memory stages and divider.\n\n  On each real ID admission, independently capture two write-first RF\n  values, one shared current EX architectural-result candidate and one\n  shared completing-MEM architectural-result candidate. Register separate\n  rs1/rs2 source tags with the owning instruction: RF, EX candidate, MEM\n  candidate, or architectural zero. Determine tags with the existing\n  EX-over-MEM-over-RF priority, nonzero destination matches, actual producer\n  advance/completion and final trap-qualified writes. Preserve conservative\n  matching of both raw encoded source fields for exact RVFI values.\n  Capture EX and MEM candidate buses without their write-eligibility muxes;\n  eligibility determines tags, never the candidate data or its write enable.\n  Ineligible candidate bits are harmless because no valid consumer selects\n  them. A load's EX address must never receive an EX-value tag.\n\n  Remove the existing resolved rs1_val/rs2_val and final alu_a/alu_b\n  storage from ID/EX. Replace those four 32-bit words with rf_rs1,\n  rf_rs2, ex_candidate and mem_candidate, plus the two small source tags.\n  Retain instruction PC, immediate, destination/source addresses, original\n  decoded controls, prediction and saved direct target. Thus candidate\n  storage replaces existing operand words rather than duplicating them.\n  Fully reset all new registers. Candidate payloads use one raw hold\n  consisting only of load-use, EX busy and dmem hold, without redirect or\n  imem-valid terms. Reset/flush/invalid-fetch policy for controls and valid\n  remains complete; correction separately invalidates ownership and wins\n  over raw holds. A correction may leave arbitrary inactive candidate\n  payload, but cannot admit, execute or retire that payload.\n\n  EX resolves architectural rs1/rs2 solely from its own registered bank\n  and tags, then selects PC/immediate ALU sources using registered controls.\n  Combine source-tag and PC/immediate selection into a shallow masked\n  selection tree; do not add a serial RF/EX/MEM mux followed by two further\n  x0 and immediate muxes. Tags and selection controls are all registered.\n  The candidate data path never traverses forward_unit eligibility before\n  entering its bank. Store data, branches, JALR, divider requests and saved\n  RVFI operands use these same resolved architectural values. EX/MEM and\n  MEM/WB retain their architectural metadata meanings. An ID/EX hold keeps\n  all candidates and tags together, so draining WB or changing memory data\n  cannot change an already admitted instruction's operands.\n\n  Keep the current accepted EX result bus, including link values and held\n  M responses, and the exact accepted-load extraction path. The new timing\n  recurrence is registered candidates/tags -> EX local selection -> ALU\n  and architectural result mux -> candidate register. Producer qualification\n  and dependency matching end at narrow tag registers in parallel. This\n  differs from r6s1: it does not enlarge a combinational ID crossbar or\n  drive its wide data mux with unregistered completion/trap eligibility.\n  It also differs from the pre-r3 pipeline: EX never reads a live regfile\n  or live MEM bypass bus before arithmetic. Only instruction-owned flops\n  feed execution.\n\n  Protect the multiplier from the new local selection prefix. Add one\n  private operand-capture step to mul_unit: accept resolved operands and\n  operation into request registers, then compute the existing shared\n  signed 33-by-33 product into its held response on the next edge. Product\n  selection and all RV32M signs remain exact. This adds one cycle per\n  multiply, while keeping the DSP input timing independent of the EX\n  source-selection mux. ALTOPS uses the same request ownership and capture\n  step with the exact existing formulas, applied to every operand pair.\n  Keep division's existing request capture/PREP/iteration/sign/response\n  protocol and its real/ALTOPS arithmetic unchanged. M requests launch\n  once, older stages drain, and responses remain stable until accepted.\n\n  An immediately dependent load still waits one bubble and then captures\n  the accepted MEM value into its candidate bank. No extra ordinary ALU,\n  branch, load or store stage or interlock is allowed. Dmem stalls retain\n  EX/MEM and ID/EX, prevent premature tags and M transfers, and suppress\n  duplicate retirement. Invalid, trapped or squashed instructions cannot\n  forward, redirect, train, launch M operations, access memory or retire.\n  Preserve decoder default-illegal validation, EBREAK-only SYSTEM legality,\n  all alignment traps and byte masks, complete architectural reset,\n  nret=1, exact top-level IO and consecutive channel-zero RVFI orders.\n  Modify no benchmark, evaluator, wrapper, contract file or other core.\n\n  Predict twelve percent fitness improvement with low confidence. This\n  final candidate-bank topology is unmeasured; the failed queue probes\n  provide negative evidence only. It relocates selection delay rather\n  than eliminating it, so ALU feedback, narrow tag qualification, local\n  branch selection or accepted-load capture may still limit Fmax. Each\n  multiply adds one cycle; the r3/r4 cycle difference suggests roughly\n  94,172 extra cycles as an approximate workload reference, not a measured\n  count for this design. At 4,390,616 cycles, acceptance needs median\n  Fmax above about 100.287 MHz and fitness above 228.405 iter/sec. Require\n  actual marker-bounded cycles and median Gowin timing; correctness or a\n  shorter logic cone alone does not establish improvement. Run unchanged\n  formal, real M arithmetic tests, exact stalled RVFI cosimulation and\n  canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/OOB/marker gates.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace ID/EX's four resolved operand/ALU words with two RF candidates\n      and shared EX/MEM candidates plus explicit rs1/rs2 source tags.\n      Retain immediate, instruction/control/PC/prediction ownership and\n      downstream architectural bundle meanings; no IO or nret change.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Independently register write-first RF and raw EX/MEM candidate buses\n      and the narrow eligibility-qualified source tags. Remove wide\n      combinational resolved-operand and final-ALU-input muxing from ID.\n      Preserve x0, source metadata, reset and full control squash; hold all\n      candidate data/tags together using correction-independent raw holds.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Produce dependency source tags with existing youngest-writer and\n      x0 priority and accepted, final-trap-qualified producer eligibility.\n      Tags are captured by ID rather than driving a wide ID data crossbar.\n      Keep raw unused source-field treatment required by RVFI comparison.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Resolve architectural sources and ALU inputs from registered banks\n      and tags through a shallow local selection structure. Use identical\n      resolved values for branches, JALR, stores, M requests and RVFI.\n      Export raw architectural-result candidates separately from narrow\n      producer eligibility. Preserve trap/recovery/training acceptance,\n      load exclusion, launch-once, response ownership and EX/MEM holds.\n  - file: rtl/mul_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Add private request operand/op registers and one capture-to-product\n      preparation step to isolate the DSP from EX's new source selection.\n      Preserve the shared signed product, exact low/high selection, every\n      real and ALTOPS formula, request rejection while busy, held responses\n      and reset. Do not reintroduce combinational multiply-to-ID forwarding.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Export raw load-use/EX-busy/dmem holds independent of redirect and\n      instruction-bus availability for candidate payload ownership.\n      Preserve architectural correction priority, one load-use bubble,\n      imem invalid-fetch handling, arithmetic draining and dmem precedence.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire raw EX/MEM candidates and captured source tags into ID and the\n      raw hold signal to candidate payload registers. Preserve the memory\n      bus, producer acceptance, retirement counter and all channel-0 RVFI\n      ports and architectural field values.\n  - file: test/test_id_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt packed ID/EX layouts and assert independent bank/tag capture,\n      reset, hold and squash ownership. Check x0, competing producers,\n      ineligible candidate changes, unavailable fetch and correction during\n      load-use/M/dmem holds without weakening existing checks.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Supply candidate-bank/tag stimuli and retain all architectural result,\n      branch/JALR alignment, store-data and exact RVFI operand assertions.\n      Exercise every source tag and PC/immediate selection, including\n      candidates that change while the owning instruction is held.\n  - file: test/test_mul_unit.py\n    description: |\n      Retain every real RV32M vector and adapt bounded latency checks for\n      the request-capture step. Check changing inputs after launch,\n      completion backpressure, back-to-back requests, reset at each phase\n      and exact signed/unsigned high-half and low-half results.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt bundle layouts and test EX/MEM/RF candidate tag priority,\n      immediate/PC selection, raw unused source fields, x0, load-to-branch,\n      load-to-M, store dependencies and immediate M-result consumers under\n      independent stalls. Preserve all exact RVFI/order/memory assertions\n      and verify no ordinary instruction gains a compulsory issue bubble.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r11s1.yaml');d=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));assert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-002-r11s1';assert d['category']=='structural';assert type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int; print('Schema valid:',p);print('Fmax threshold:',4390616*218.36*1.046/1e7)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
