{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261002-002-r5s1','hyp-20261002-002-r7s1','hyp-20261002-002-r8s1','hyp-20261002-002-r9s1','hyp-20261003-002-r11s1'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ids:\n  h=d['hypothesis']\n  print(d['id'])\n  print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  if d.get('implementation_notes'): print(d['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261002-002-r5s1','hyp-20261002-002-r7s1','hyp-20261002-002-r8s1','hyp-20261002-002-r9s1','hyp-20261003-002-r11s1'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ids:\n  h=d['hypothesis']\n  print(d['id'])\n  print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  if d.get('implementation_notes'): print(d['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-002-r5s1\nMake the existing one-register multiplier a pipelined EX-to-MEM producer.\nLaunch a legal multiply exactly when its ID/EX instruction advances into\nEX/MEM. Capture the existing execute/RVFI metadata in EX/MEM on that same\nedge and the selected architectural product in mul_unit's response\nregister. MEM consumes these matching registers together, selects the\nregistered multiply answer as its architectural result, and forwards it\nthrough the existing completing-MEM bypass. Remove multiply responses\nfrom the advancing-EX bypass and remove multiply from blocking EX busy.\nDivision retains its existing blocking request/response implementation.\nThis is one structural change to multiply issue and completion ownership;\nretain the predictor, fetch topology and ordinary RV32I pipeline latency.\n\nRegister explicit multiply and divide operation-class bits with the\ndecoded ID/EX controls. Use those bits for stage routing and interlocks\ninstead of rediscovering classes from alu_op on the EX-to-ID feedback\npath. Extend the one-bubble load-use rule to an immediately dependent\nmultiply consumer: an EX multiply has no current EX bypass value, but\nthe next cycle its registered MEM result can be captured into ID/EX.\nTreat multiply dependencies on branch/JALR operands, effective addresses\nand store data exactly like other architectural register dependencies.\nPreserve EX-over-MEM-over-write-first-RF priority and x0 semantics.\nIndependent successors advance without a multiply stall; dependent\nsuccessors require one bubble, so no instruction requires more multiply\ndelay than the current implementation in the zero-wait case.\n\nPermit mul_unit to accept a new request on the same edge its previous\nresponse is consumed, giving consecutive independent multiplies one\nresult per cycle. Request acceptance must be a legal EX advance, response\nconsumption must be the matching EX/MEM multiply's MEM acceptance, and\ntheir controls must not form a combinational ready/valid loop. A dmem\nstall freezes EX/MEM and younger stages and proh\n…[22341 of 25413 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0ad57af23c3e979c]…\net=1, exact top-level IO and consecutive channel-zero RVFI orders.\nModify no benchmark, evaluator, wrapper, contract file or other core.\n\nPredict twelve percent fitness improvement with low confidence. This\nfinal candidate-bank topology is unmeasured; the failed queue probes\nprovide negative evidence only. It relocates selection delay rather\nthan eliminating it, so ALU feedback, narrow tag qualification, local\nbranch selection or accepted-load capture may still limit Fmax. Each\nmultiply adds one cycle; the r3/r4 cycle difference suggests roughly\n94,172 extra cycles as an approximate workload reference, not a measured\ncount for this design. At 4,390,616 cycles, acceptance needs median\nFmax above about 100.284 MHz and fitness above 228.405 iter/sec. Require\nactual marker-bounded cycles and median Gowin timing; correctness or a\nshorter logic cone alone does not establish improvement. Run unchanged\nformal, real M arithmetic tests, exact stalled RVFI cosimulation and\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/UART/OOB/marker gates.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
