{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261002-002-r6s1','hyp-20261003-002-r11s1','hyp-20261002-002-r8s1','hyp-20261002-002-r9s1'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r['id'] in ids:\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(r['id'],r['title'])\n  print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-002-r6s1 Replace the shared EX bypass bus with direct operand forwarding\nReplace the centralized EX-result bypass bus and serial ID input muxes\nwith an operand-local, one-hot forwarding crossbar feeding the existing\nID/EX registers directly. This is one structural change to result and\noperand flow; retain all pipeline boundaries, the predictor, blocking\nmultiplier/divider timing, load-use interlock and instruction throughput.\n\nEX exports separate raw candidate buses for the ordinary ALU result,\nPC+4 link value, registered multiply response and registered divide\nresponse. Export the matching destination and qualified write enable\nfor each candidate. Ordinary ALU instructions must reach younger\noperand registers without first passing through the jump-versus-ALU\nand multiply/divide-versus-ordinary shared result muxes. EX/MEM still\ncaptures the same selected architectural result for normal completion.\nRemoving these muxes from forwarding must not duplicate ALU arithmetic\nor alter the actual RV32M units and their exact ALTOPS formulas.\n\nFor each raw ID source address, compute mutually exclusive candidate\nselects in parallel with result production. Require a nonzero source,\nmatching nonzero destination, valid producer, permitted trap-adjusted\nregister write and actual producer advance. The EX class selects must\nbe mutually exclusive: ordinary ALU versus link versus accepted multiply\nversus accepted divide. Completed arithmetic responses use the saved\nlaunch destination and metadata. A load has no EX value. A busy,\ninvalid, trapped or stalled instruction is never an eligible producer.\nMEM is eligible only for its actual accepted architectural result,\nincluding the existing sign/zero-extended load extraction; EX wins\nover MEM and MEM wins over the existing write-first register-file read.\n\nUse these early selects directly in balanced mask-and-OR datapaths,\nrather than encoding a selection and rebuilding nested priority muxes.\nForm rs1_val and rs2_val from the independent candidates for branches,\nJALR, store d\n…[18090 of 21162 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:882f08bf4fcf9844]…\net=1, exact top-level IO and consecutive channel-zero RVFI orders.\nModify no benchmark, evaluator, wrapper, contract file or other core.\n\nPredict twelve percent fitness improvement with low confidence. This\nfinal candidate-bank topology is unmeasured; the failed queue probes\nprovide negative evidence only. It relocates selection delay rather\nthan eliminating it, so ALU feedback, narrow tag qualification, local\nbranch selection or accepted-load capture may still limit Fmax. Each\nmultiply adds one cycle; the r3/r4 cycle difference suggests roughly\n94,172 extra cycles as an approximate workload reference, not a measured\ncount for this design. At 4,390,616 cycles, acceptance needs median\nFmax above about 100.284 MHz and fitness above 228.405 iter/sec. Require\nactual marker-bounded cycles and median Gowin timing; correctness or a\nshorter logic cone alone does not establish improvement. Run unchanged\nformal, real M arithmetic tests, exact stalled RVFI cosimulation and\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/UART/OOB/marker gates.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
