{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +735.07%): Moving combinational DIV/REM (`/` and `%`) out of the shared ALU into a registered iterative divider removes their timing cost from ordinary instructions, letting Fmax gains outweigh CoreMark's added divide stalls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (regression, +7.45%): Registering elastic IF/ID removes redirect-to-decode feedback, but the added taken-branch refill cycle raised CoreMark cycles 6.45%, limiting a 14.39% Fmax gain to 7.45% fitness.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 (improvement, +13.63%): Instruction-qualified BHT/JAL prediction removes recurring CoreMark control bubbles, while saved direct targets enable direction-XOR \n…[5266 of 8338 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:30d0a79cc4d53b5a]…\nfrom 93.817 to 78.139 MHz.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r14s0 (regression, -4.28%): Predecoding ALU result selects and M/forwarding controls leaves registered `divide_class` feeding a 12-cell EX/forwarding-to-ID operand path, so removing binary ALU result decode alone cannot break the control feedback recurrence (median Fmax fell from 93.817 to 89.803 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles).\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r14s1 (regression, +1.58%): A four-entry circular fetch queue with empty fall-through overlaps fetch with ID holds and avoids correction-refill bubbles, but frontend elasticity must preserve decode/operand timing: 7.05% fewer CoreMark cycles were mostly offset by median Fmax falling from 93.817 to 88.583 MHz.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r14s2 (regression, -16.06%): Deleting the 128-entry BHT for static backward-taken prediction saves 256 FFs but leaves `alu_op`-to-MUL/forwarding-to-ID operand feedback critical, so median Fmax fell from 93.817 to 79.787 MHz while CoreMark cycles rose only 1.32%.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
