{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in {'hyp-20261002-002-r3s1','hyp-20261002-002-r2s1'}:\n  print(d['id'],d['title'])\n  h=d.get('hypothesis')\n  print(h.get('hypothesis') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  for c in d.get('changes',[]): print(c['file'], c['description'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 Register the fetch-to-decode boundary to break redirect feedback\nTurn the combinational IF/ID bundle into a one-entry registered,\nelastic fetch-to-decode stage carrying instruction, its exact PC, and\nvalid. Decode and both register-file read addresses must depend only\non the registered instruction, never on a combinational redirect,\nbus-ready signal, or injected NOP. Keep ID/EX, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, the\nexisting forwarding paths, and the blocking iterative divider. This\nis one structural change: establish a real register boundary between\ninstruction fetch and decode to sever the redirect-to-decode feedback.\n\nUse explicit consume and refill events. A buffered instruction is\nconsumed only when decode can advance without a load-use interlock,\ndmem hold, divider occupancy, or redirect. When the slot is empty or\nis being consumed, a ready instruction-memory response may refill it\non the same edge; advance the fetch PC by four exactly on that refill.\nWhen full and blocked, retain the buffered PC/instruction and fetch PC.\nWhen empty, allow a refill even if downstream is blocked. io_imemReady\nqualifies only new fetch acceptance: a buffered instruction remains\navailable to decode even if the instruction bus subsequently stalls.\nIf decode consumes without a refill, clear buffer valid and inject\nsubsequent invalid bubbles rather than replaying that instruction.\n\nKeep raw registered instruction payload independent of squash. On an\naccepted EX redirect, set fetch PC to the resolved target, invalidate\nIF/ID and the younger ID/EX instruction, and suppress same-edge refill\nfrom the old fetch address. Redirect overrides fetch backpressure;\nEX must still defer resolution while an older dmem operation holds it.\nThe target is fetched on a later ready cycle. Both younger slots must\nbe killed, including when the buffered wrong-path instruction is a\nstore, divide, or illegal encoding. Preserve misalignment traps and\nnever launch a divider or memory side effect from an invalid slot.\nDecode legality still defaults to ill\n…[12358 of 15430 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e689c8b060663e93]…\nble. Make only handshake\nadjustments needed to keep producer availability consistent with\ninstruction admission; do not add stalls for ordinary dependencies.\n\ntest/test_pipeline.py Extend dependency tests for current EX, MEM, and WB values, multiple\nsimultaneous writers, x0, ALU/multiply chains, links, branch/JALR\noperands, load addresses, and store data. Verify that immediate\nload consumers still require exactly one bubble without bus stalls\nand ordinary dependencies sustain one issue per cycle. Cover a\nyounger EX write shadowing an older MEM/WB write, signed subword\nloads, misaligned/trapped producers, independently stalled buses,\ndivider completion/alternation, held instructions, corrections,\nand wrong-path stores. Check exact RVFI operands/results/PCs,\nconsecutive order, no duplicate memory effects, and bounded\nprogress. Keep all existing arithmetic, legality, prediction,\ndivider, and pipeline assertions and run the unchanged formal/cosim\ngates; the arithmetic implementations themselves are not altered.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
