{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_54", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r15s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r15s1',\n 'title':'Separate the fast ALU bypass from registered M-extension completion',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 218.36 iterations/sec at 93.817 MHz and 4,296,444 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The structural r12s1 result stage removed live EX forwarding but increased cycles 19.71 percent. R5s1 and r7s1 pipelined multiplication into MEM yet left ordinary bypass and recovery critical. R14s0 predecoded execution classes but retained the shared completion network. The remaining opportunity is to separate ordinary result production from blocking M completion, rather than register every result or repeat a wider frontend queue.\n\nDuring this proposal turn, an isolated one-slot fall-through fetch probe passed stalled RVFI comparison and canonical CoreMark at 4,057,628 cycles, but Gowin median Fmax was only 78.332 MHz (193.05 iterations/sec). Reject that frontend probe. A first selective M-completion probe passed selftest and all three random-program RVFI comparisons, plus canonical CoreMark at 4,331,635 cycles: only 0.819 percent more than the champion. Its median Fmax was 86.519 MHz, so it also failed acceptance. Its timing path still traversed branch comparison and shared EX/M-unit control into ID/EX clock enables. The proposed topology must therefore separate M launch/completion qualification from branch-trap logic as well as remove M results from the fast bypass. These failed probes provide negative evidence, not a claimed improvement.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Partition execute result ownership into an ordinary fast ALU/link bypass and a registered M-extension completion lane. Preserve the current pipeline depth, frontend predictor, ID's registered resolved operands and final ALU inputs, memory interface, and blocking multiplier/divider arithmetic. The architectural change is that an accepted M response only enters EX/MEM and becomes visible to younger operands through completing MEM; it never competes on the live EX-to-ID bypass bus. Ordinary ALU/link dependencies still issue without new bubbles. Only an immediately dependent M-result consumer gains one interlock cycle.\n\nRegister three validated scheduling bits with ctrl_t in ID/EX: is_multiply, is_divide, and early_write. Derive the M classes only from a decoder-validated RV32M OP encoding; multiplication uses funct3[2]=0 and division/remainder funct3[2]=1. early_write means a legal decoded register writer that is neither a load nor an M operation. Clear all three with the entire control bundle on squash or invalid admission, hold them with the original operands, and reset fully. Do not replace default-illegal decoding or accept reserved instruction encodings. These bits describe ownership, not speculative producer availability.\n\nExpose only the ordinary ALU result or PC+4 link value on EX bypass_data and the current instruction's rd on bypass_rd. Remove the multiply/divide response mux, saved-M-destination mux, and M-transfer checks from that bus. Qualify an early producer from its registered early_write, valid, nonzero rd, reset and older dmem acceptance. Apply the jump-specific low-bit alignment check to link writes. Do not put the general conditional-branch comparator, ctrl_with_trap, M ready/valid, or global arithmetic busy into early-producer qualification: decoded early_write is mutually exclusive with loads, conditional branches and M occupancy. Preserve all architectural trap metadata independently, including branch/jump alignment faults and link-write suppression on a misaligned jump.\n\nMake the M lane's request and response control independent of the general branch-comparison/trap cone. A request requires the valid registered legal M-class bit, no reset or older dmem hold, and that unit's request-ready. Those class bits already imply a validated M writer; do not gate launch with branch-adjusted ctrl_with_trap. A response requires the matching current M owner, saved launch ownership, result-valid and downstream acceptance. Keep operation-specific req_op for arithmetic, but use registered class bits for occupancy and completion routing instead of repeatedly decoding alu_op. Use the registered decoded legal ownership controls for any additional qualification; no branch_cond, branch_taken, misalign_branch or branch-adjusted control may feed M request, transfer, busy or response-ready. Preserve branch/JAL/JALR resolve, prediction correction and training timing for their own instructions. This separation is required: the first selective-bypass probe still failed because it left shared qualification intact.\n\nKeep each unit's current accepted-launch snapshot, exact architectural operand/PC/instruction metadata, bounded arithmetic, and stable held-response protocol. While M is busy, hold ID/EX and fetch, drain older MEM/WB work, and insert side-effect-free EX/MEM bubbles. On result acceptance, place the saved instruction and result into EX/MEM exactly once. A stalled older memory request prevents both launch and response transfer. The M instruction retires at its existing boundary; delaying its consumer does not add an M retirement stage. Preserve both real arithmetic and every exact RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS stand-in, including zero divisors, signed overflow and high-half products. Modify neither arithmetic datapath nor unit latency in this hypothesis.\n\nExtend the current unavailable-producer interlock from EX loads to EX multiply/divide owners. Conservatively match both raw younger source fields, excluding x0, for exact RVFI operand values even when an encoded source is unused by execution. While M is busy, occupancy holds take precedence over dependency bubbling. On the M completion edge, a matching consumer holds fetch and receives an ID/EX bubble while the M result advances into EX/MEM. On the following accepted MEM cycle, that consumer captures the registered M result through the existing MEM bypass. An independent successor may enter ID/EX on the completion edge. Keep the existing one-bubble load-use rule, EX-over-MEM-over-write-first-RF priority, accepted load extraction, held operands after WB drains, and correct overlapping destination behavior. Never forward a younger unavailable producer's stale RF value or a load's effective address.\n\nPredict eight percent fitness improvement with low confidence. The first selective-completion probe establishes only the small cycle cost, not a timing gain. At its 4,331,635-cycle count, acceptance requires median Fmax above 98.937 MHz and fitness above 228.405 iterations/sec. Approximately 102.15 MHz at that count would yield eight percent greater fitness. Removing M response/destination selection and operand-dependent branch qualification from the fast bypass/completion network is intended to supply that gain; ordinary ALU arithmetic, branch correction, accepted-load capture or physical routing can still dominate. Measure the final median over all Gowin placement options and actual bracketed cycles. A shorter RTL path, fewer LUTs or one favorable placement does not establish success.\n\nPreserve nret=1 and exact top-level memory/RVFI IO, one channel-zero retirement per instruction with consecutive orders, decoder legality and EBREAK-only SYSTEM handling, alignment traps and byte masks, complete reset, memory handshakes, no wrong-path side effects, and all hard invariants. Change no evaluator, benchmark, wrapper, contract file or other core. Require the unchanged formal and stalled RVFI cosimulation gates, existing real/ALTOPS arithmetic tests, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART literal/OOB/MMIO-marker validation. The final topology still needs full formal and directed-unit validation after implementation.''',\n 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add registered validated early_write, is_multiply and is_divide scheduling bits to ctrl_t. Keep existing architectural bundle fields and meanings, stage count, nret and IO. Document that only ordinary ALU/link results are available before EX/MEM; M results become available on accepted MEM completion.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Generate scheduling tags only from validated decoded controls and RV32M encoding. Capture them with the owning instruction, clear them with all control bits on reset/bubble/squash, and hold them with resolved operands. Preserve every existing RF/EX/MEM operand choice, final ALU input capture, raw source-address convention and payload/control reset policy.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Partition fast ALU/link bypass from registered M response completion. Remove M result and saved-destination choices from early bypass, use registered early_write and jump-only alignment qualification, and keep branch trap metadata separate. Drive M request/busy/transfer/ready only from validated registered M ownership, unit handshakes, saved launch validity and downstream acceptance; remove general branch-adjusted qualification from those controls. Preserve launch-once snapshots, existing unit arithmetic/latency, held responses, draining bubbles and exactly-once EX/MEM transfer.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'''Treat a valid EX load or M owner as an unavailable producer for matching younger raw sources. Retain dmem/EX-busy precedence, inserting the dependent-M bubble only when its result can advance into EX/MEM, then releasing the consumer to accepted MEM forwarding. Preserve ordinary ALU/link issue rate, existing one-bubble load-use, redirect priority, full side-effect-control squash and no duplicate retirement.'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire registered load/M unavailability into the hazard interlock and the narrowed fast EX bypass into the existing ID forwarding network. Keep the current MEM completion bypass, WB/RF path, all frontend behavior, exact top-level ports and RVFI retirement/order wiring.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_id_stage.py','description':'''Update packed control widths/masks without weakening existing reset, hold, payload or squash assertions. Check legal multiply/divide classification across all eight M operations, early-write exclusion for M/loads/branches/illegal encodings, link eligibility and complete tag clearing on invalid or squashed admission.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'''Update control layouts and decoder-consistent tags. Retain exact result, trap, saved-operand, PC, hold and reset checks for real/ALTOPS execution. Verify no EX bypass for a waiting or completed M operation, exactly one M launch/transfer, stable held responses during dmem stalls, ordinary/link bypass eligibility and misaligned-jump suppression.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Update packed layouts and explicitly expect one added dependency bubble only for immediate M consumers. Retain all exact architectural/RVFI/order/memory assertions. Cover dependent and independent successors of all M operations, mixed/back-to-back M units, x0, unused raw source fields, overlapping writers, M-result branch/JALR/address/store consumers, loads preceding M, dmem holds at launch/completion, imem stalls, reset, prediction corrections, and wrong-path M/store suppression. Require unchanged ordinary ALU/link throughput and one-bubble load-use. Run canonical marker-bounded CoreMark and vendor timing; do not weaken correctness or liveness gates.'''}\n ]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,allow_unicode=True,default_flow_style=False,width=88))\nimport json,jsonschema\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised structural hypothesis written and schema validated:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
