{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis'); h=h if isinstance(h,dict) else d\n  print('\\\\n'+d['id']+' '+d['title'])\n  print({k:d.get(k) for k in ['outcome','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','lut4','ff']})\n  print('proposal:',h.get('hypothesis','')[:2600])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nhyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Small tagged target predictor eliminates recurrent control-flow bubbles\n{'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 13.02, 'delta_pct': 7.43, 'fmax_mhz': 5.467, 'cycles': 4197517, 'lut4': 12795, 'ff': 685}\nproposal: Add a 64-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer with an integrated\ntwo-bit saturating direction counter. Index with fetch PC[7:2]; retain\nthe full PC[31:8] tag, a word-aligned 30-bit target, a conditional versus\nunconditional type bit, and a valid bit. A matching unconditional entry\npredicts its cached target; a conditional entry predicts that target\nwhen the counter MSB is set. A miss or not-taken prediction chooses pc+4.\nInclude JAL and JALR in the same predictor, using the last resolved\ntarget for JALR, without adding a return stack or global history.\n\nCarry the exact predicted next PC alongside each accepted instruction\nthrough IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX compute the architectural next PC using\nthe existing forwarded operands, branch comparison, JALR bit-zero\nclearing, and misalignment/trap rules. Redirect and flush only when a\nvalid instruction advances out of EX and its actual next PC differs\nfrom its saved prediction. Check this equality for every instruction,\nincluding non-control and trapped instructions, so stale entries cannot\nsilently skip architectural instructions. RVFI pc_wdata always reports\nthe resolved next PC, never the prediction. Correctly predicted taken\ncontrol flow captures the target instruction normally without a bubble.\n\nTrain once per valid EX advance, never during dmem holds or bubbles.\nAllocate only decoded legal control-flow instructions with aligned\ntargets; initialize conditional counters to weakly taken or weakly\nnot-taken according to the resolved direction, and saturate on later\ntag-matching updates. Unconditional entries always predict taken and\nupdate their target on each resolution. Invalidate a matching stale\nentry when a non-control instruction or trapping control flow resolves\nthere. Clear valid bits on reset; other table fields need not reset and\n…[22394 of 25466 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:95f3e622d2d16fca]…\nvalid transaction; reset discards any queued event and resets the table. The table may update during a later pipeline hold because its transaction belongs to an already accepted instruction.\n\nImplement the BHT as four explicitly generated banks of thirty-two two-bit counter payloads. Select bank with index[6:5] and row with index[4:0], retaining PC[8:2] for both fetch and training. Each bank has a 32-bit resettable valid vector; its counter payload array has no reset so Gowin can infer asynchronous-read, synchronous-write LUT RAM. An invalid fetch or update row behaves as 2'b01. The first queued taken update therefore writes 2'b10, and a not-taken update writes 2'b00. Read fetch and training rows independently. Select completed bank counters for fetch, parallel with the existing target additions. Payload writes are unconditional on saturation whenever !reset && train_valid_q selects the bank; compute next_counter combinationally from the current valid-qualified row and write even when the saturated value is \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
