{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '240,510p' tools/eval/gowin.py; sed -n '1,145p' cores/bench/test/conftest.py; sed -n '1,90p' bench/programs/Makefile; rg -n 'snapshot|counter|stale|hyp-20261003|reference profiling' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-250\"", "aggregated_output": "\"\"\"pytest configuration for cocotb unit tests.\n\nAdds this directory to sys.path so test_*.py modules can `import _helpers`\nduring pytest collection. Cocotb sets PYTHONPATH separately for the\nsimulator-side import.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n_HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nif str(_HERE) not in sys.path:\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HERE))\nCROSS   := riscv32-unknown-elf\nCC      := $(CROSS)-gcc\n\n# CoreMark build knobs. Defaults are the canonical EEMBC reporting config\n# (2K data size, -O3); tools/eval/fpga.py's COREMARK_EXPECTED CRCs assume\n# exactly these defaults.\n# - COREMARK_SIZE: TOTAL_DATA_SIZE in coremark.h (#ifndef-guarded). 2000 is\n#   the EEMBC canonical reporting config (used by VexRiscv et al.); 6000\n#   makes the matrix/list/state working sets ~3x larger so the score is\n#   harder to inflate via register-resident working sets.\n# - COREMARK_OPT: gcc optimization. -O3 is the standard CoreMark publication\n#   flag; -O2 is what the V0 baseline used.\n# Defaults match VexRiscv's published CoreMark methodology so the\n# orchestrator's fitness number is directly comparable to public CoreMark\n# results. To override (e.g. for the prior 6K -O2 V0 baseline):\n#     make bench/programs/coremark.elf COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O2\nCOREMARK_SIZE ?= 2000\nCOREMARK_OPT  ?= -O3\n\nCFLAGS  := -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 $(COREMARK_OPT) -static -nostartfiles \\\n           -specs=nano.specs -specs=nosys.specs \\\n           -T bench/programs/link.ld -Ibench/programs/coremark \\\n           -Ibench/programs/coremark/baremetal \\\n           -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=$(COREMARK_SIZE)\n\nSRCS := bench/programs/coremark/core_list_join.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_main.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_matrix.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_state.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_util.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/baremetal/core_portme.c \\\n        bench/programs/crt0.S\n\nbench/programs/coremark.elf: $(SRCS)\n\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $^ -o $@\n\nbench/programs/selftest.elf: bench/programs/selftest.S\n\n…[3873 of 6945 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:61bcc38f7ec024f4]…\nr-bounded CoreMark \n25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Register lookahead direction lookup without adding a fetch stage\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 218.36 iterations/sec at 93.817 MHz with 4,296,444\\nmarker-bounded CoreMark cy\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Update saturating branch counters locally without a training read mux\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 218.36 iterations/sec at 93.817 MHz with 4,296,444\\nmeasured CoreMark cyc\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple predicted fetch with an independently written circular instruction queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion scores 218.36 iter/sec at 93.817 MHz with 4,296,444\\nmarker-bounded\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Four-bank bimodal predictor with registered training transactions\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The 218.36 iter/sec champion already predicts direct branches and JAL. Reference profiling finds \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"The hypothesis file is written and validates against the schema. It proposes carrying the counter observed at fetch into EX, so training can use that registered snapshot and eliminate the second table-read mux. I’ll check a temporary prototype with Gowin; closely spaced branches that alias may learn from stale snapshots, so accuracy remains a tradeoff.\n"}}
