{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r10s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261003-003-r10s2\ntitle: \"Train the direction predictor from registered fetch counter snapshots\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's instruction-qualified 128-entry bimodal predictor already\n  removes most direct-control bubbles. Folded indexing saved only 0.29%\n  of CoreMark cycles, while one-bit agree prediction, registered lookahead,\n  local counter updates and queued banked training all missed acceptance.\n  Consequently this proposal targets the predictor's training circuit,\n  rather than assuming that marginally better direction accuracy can\n  provide the required 4.6% fitness gain. The current EX training interface\n  performs a second variable-index table read, saturation and writeback.\n  Sampling that update operand during fetch can remove this entire training\n  read mux while retaining two-bit hysteresis and the present recovery\n  latency. Unlike the previous local-update attempt, there is no generated\n  per-entry saturation logic or read of a counter on the training side.\nhypothesis: |\n  Carry the two-bit BHT counter observed by an accepted fetch through\n  IF/ID and ID/EX. On the existing legal, aligned conditional-branch EX\n  acceptance event, compute the next saturating counter exclusively from\n  this registered snapshot and the actual branch outcome, then write it\n  unconditionally to the original PC[8:2] table index. Remove every read of\n  counters[branch_index] from the training path, including saturation\n  tests. Keep the 128x2 resettable table, weakly-not-taken reset value,\n  asynchronous current-PC lookup and original low-PC index unchanged.\n  Do not add training queues, banks, validity arrays or lookup registers.\n\n  Use explicit two-bit transition logic: taken maps 00,01,10,11 to\n  01,10,11,11; not taken maps them to 00,00,01,10. Each accepted branch\n  performs one variable-index write even when the value is saturated.\n  Nonblocking semantics give an accepted fetch its pre-write snapshot if\n  a concurrent branch trains the same row. The snapshot is the counter\n  associate\n…[3282 of 6354 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:46cb1a3208ed3b07]…\nery unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the two-bit EX counter snapshot to IF alongside the existing\n      branch_update, branch_index and branch_outcome signals.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Add a cycle model for fetch snapshots and training writes. Cover all\n      eight transitions, reset, saturation, aliasing, concurrent same-row\n      fetch/write and unconditional saturated writes; assert that supplied\n      snapshots, rather than the live training-index table value, determine\n      updates. Check legal instruction and alignment prediction gates.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise overlapping aliased branches and short loops, holds between\n      snapshot capture and resolution, independent bus backpressure,\n      load-use bubbles, M occupancy and corrections. Assert exact RVFI\n      PCs, resolved next PCs, results, traps and consecutive order, one\n      training write per accepted branch, and no wrong-path side effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
