{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n e=json.loads(line)\n if e.get('category')=='predictor' and e['id'] in ['hyp-20261002-003-r5s2','hyp-20261002-003-r6s2','hyp-20261002-003-r4s2']:\n  print(e['id'],e['title']); print(e['hypothesis']['hypothesis']); print(e.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r4s2 Compact banked direction prediction on the operand-retimed champion\nReplace only the direction-table implementation with a 128-entry,\ntwo-bit saturating bimodal predictor organized as eight banks of\nsixteen rows. Use the same seven-bit index in fetch and EX training:\nPC[8:2] XOR PC[12:6]. Select bank with index[6:4] and row with\nindex[3:0]. Higher PC bits reduce interference between branches that\ncollide in the current low-PC-only table. Preserve the existing\ninstruction-qualified conditional prediction and aligned direct JAL\nprediction; JALR continues to resolve in EX.\n\nImplement each bank's counter payload as an asynchronous-read,\nsynchronous-write 16x2 array without reset. Reset only a sixteen-bit\nvalidity vector per bank. A row whose validity is zero behaves as\n2'b01, exactly the champion's weakly-not-taken reset value, regardless\nof uninitialized payload bits. On the first accepted update, write\n2'b10 for taken or 2'b00 for not taken and set validity. Otherwise\nsaturate the live counter at the training row. Separate payload writes\nfrom validity reset so Gowin can infer small distributed RAMs. Support\nsimultaneous fetch and training at different rows; do not substitute\nthe saved prediction for the live update counter or add a combinational\nEX-outcome bypass into fetch. No reset sweep or registered lookup.\nCompute a combinational next_counter per bank, defaulting to its live\nupdate counter and overriding it with the saturating increment or\ndecrement. The payload always_ff writes next_counter whenever\n!reset && branch_update && branch_index[6:4] selects that bank,\nincluding unchanged saturated values. A separate always_ff resets\nvalidity or marks the updated row valid. Use portable arrays rather\nthan vendor primitives or RAM attributes.\n\nKeep table lookup parallel with the existing direct-target and PC+4\nadditions, selecting completed addresses. Keep the exact accepted\npredicted_taken bit and instruction-derived direct_target in ID/EX,\ndirection-XOR recovery, and resolved architectural PC \n…[7880 of 10952 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e939a8e65ff269a8]…\nraining; younger imem bits must not train EX.\n\nPreserve correction priority over younger imem/load-use stalls, complete\ncontrol/valid/predicted_taken clearing on squash, payload retention under\nstalls, real RV32M arithmetic and ALTOPS behavior, decoded trap rules,\nwrong-path side-effect suppression, nret=1 IO, consecutive RVFI order\nand resolved architectural pc_next. Do not change arithmetic, forwarding,\nmemory handshakes, benchmark or evaluation files.\n\nExpect roughly six percent higher fitness primarily from lower predictor\nstate and elimination of the training read/mutate cone, with a small\naccuracy cost possible because there is no two-bit hysteresis. Compare\nmarker-bounded canonical CoreMark cycles and median Gowin Fmax with the\nchampion; the product must exceed 228.40456 iter/sec. Validate exact\nRVFI PC sequences, opposite-bias aliasing, loop exits/re-entry, reset,\nstalled training and wrong-path stores, then unchanged formal/cosim\ngates, all canonical CRCs, the validation literal and MMIO brackets.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
