{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],d.get('fitness'),d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'))\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('id') in ['hyp-20261002-003-r6s2','hyp-20261002-003-r8s2','hyp-20261002-003-r9s2']:\n  print('\\\\n',d['id'],d.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 Small tagged target predictor eliminates recurrent control-flow bubbles 13.02 5.467 4197517\nhyp-20261002-003-r2s2 Instruction-qualified direction prediction with short recovery control 115.0 48.328 4202272\nhyp-20261002-003-r3s2 Banked LUT-RAM direction predictor with folded PC indexing 125.61 52.631 4190183\nhyp-20261002-003-r4s2 Compact banked direction prediction on the operand-retimed champion 175.05 73.351 4190183\nhyp-20261002-003-r5s2 Local prediction squash clears control while freeing payload registers 218.36 93.817 4296444\nhyp-20261002-003-r6s2 One-bit agree prediction removes the counter training lookup 205.18 88.547 4315581\nhyp-20261002-003-r7s2 Remove misprediction correction from payload stall clock enables 202.97 87.205 4296444\nhyp-20261002-003-r8s2 Register lookahead direction lookup without adding a fetch stage 192.17 82.615 4298982\nhyp-20261002-003-r9s2 Four-bank bimodal predictor with registered training transactions 190.67 81.919 4296444\nhyp-20261003-003-r10s2 Register predictor-event eligibility before execution 209.66 90.078 4296444\nhyp-20261003-003-r11s2 Resolve return bubbles with a small EX-trained return stack 210.39 90.089 4282066\n\n hyp-20261002-003-r6s2 Replace the resettable 128x2 bimodal counters with a resettable 128x1\nagree table, retaining the PC[8:2] fetch and training index. A branch's\nstatic bias is its B-immediate sign bit, instr[31]: backward branches\npredict taken and forward branches predict not taken. Each entry records\nwhether the most recently resolved indexed branch agreed with its own\nstatic bias. Reset entries to one, meaning agreement. Predict direction\nas bias when the indexed bit is one, or the inverse bias when it is zero.\nThis reduces destructive aliasing between oppositely biased branches\nwhile halving table state. Its main physical advantage is that training\nsimply writes (actual_taken == resolved_instruction[31]); it never reads\nthe old entry, increments, decrements, or tests counter saturation.\n\nKeep the existing instruction qualification, \n…[10123 of 13195 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d475783516d2c515]…\n No benchmark, contract, wrapper, tool or other-core change.\n\nExpect approximately six percent higher fitness if decoupling predictor training raises Fmax beyond the 4.6 percent acceptance margin, with a small warmup/aliasing cycle cost possible from delayed learning. This is a low-confidence forecast for the unmeasured four-bank refinement; none of the measured related prototypes cleared acceptance. Confidence is low because the unchanged ALU/forwarding and branch-recovery cones may still limit timing. Require measured median Fmax_Hz times 10 divided by marker-bounded CoreMark cycles above 228.40456 iter/sec; at unchanged cycles this needs more than 98.133 MHz. Test same-index consecutive updates, alternating outcomes, saturation, aliasing, reset with a pending event, and a pending write coincident with every hold/correction cause. Verify exactly one applied write per accepted branch and the delayed predictor's exact predictions, then unchanged full formal/cosim gates and all canonical CRC/UART/MMIO checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
