{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_28", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('id') in ['hyp-20261002-002-r8s1','hyp-20261002-002-r9s1','hyp-20261003-003-r10s2','hyp-20261002-001-r9s0']:\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'delta',d.get('delta_pct'),'fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),'crit',d.get('critical_path'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis','');print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('NOTES',d.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-002-r8s1 Resolve control-flow recovery from registered EX/MEM transactions fitness 217.64 delta -0.33 fmax 94.984 cycles 4364348 crit {'from': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.alu_a_23_s0', 'to': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -6.284, 'arrival_ns': 16.579, 'logic_cells': 10, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nMove control-flow correction and branch-predictor training from advancing\nEX to acceptance of the existing EX/MEM transaction in MEM. EX still\ncomputes conditional-branch direction, JAL/JALR destinations, alignment\nfaults and architectural next-PC, but registers the correction-needed bit,\ncorrection target, branch update enable/index and actual outcome alongside\nthat instruction in EX/MEM. Fetch, decode squash and BHT writes consume\nonly these registered records. This is one structural change to the\ncontrol-transfer resolution boundary, not a new predictor or another\nmultiply pipeline. Retain the present two-bit direction predictor, its\nindexing and reset state, direct-JAL prediction, fetch topology, ordinary\nALU latency, existing operand forwarding, and blocking RV32M units.\n\nA valid accepted EX/MEM control transfer emits its recovery and training\nevent exactly once. Use MEM acceptance, not merely occupancy; an older\ndmem hold cannot replay an event or let it overtake an older transaction.\nCompute the saved mismatch with the current direction-XOR policy: actual\nnonsequential execution is legal, aligned taken branch or legal aligned\njump; a mismatch also restores sequential execution after a misaligned\npredicted target. Carry the correct architectural pc_next and final trap\ncontrols for RVFI independently of whether a redirect is necessary.\nPredictor training uses the current counter at the saved branch index,\npreserving saturation and updating only a legal aligned conditional branch.\n\nOn registered MEM correction, redirect PC and squash both younger slots:\nthe current ID/EX instruction and the raw fetched instruction. Suppress\nthat younger EX instruction's EX/MEM advance, bypass write \n…[15463 of 18535 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:692411835af04c2e]…\nstack, a target table, a\ntraining queue or another recovery stage. This single predictor angle\nmoves static event eligibility to ID and separates prediction-event\nacceptance from unrelated M completion.\n\nExpect six percent higher fitness through Fmax at unchanged CoreMark\ncycles, with low confidence because ALU-result and load forwarding\nremain competing paths. At 4,296,444 marker-bounded cycles acceptance\nrequires more than approximately 98.133 MHz median Gowin Fmax and\nfitness above 228.40456 iter/sec. Require an identical prediction,\ntraining and retirement trace for a fixed program and ready sequence;\nany added recovery cycle is a failure of this hypothesis. Validate the\nregistered eligibility against decoder legality and control class,\nincluding all M operations and reserved encodings, then run the\nunchanged formal, stalled RVFI cosimulation and canonical CoreMark\nCRC, UART-validation, MMIO-bracket and bounded-memory gates. Keep\nnret=1, architectural operand retention and consecutive RVFI order.\n\nNOTES \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
